\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Up Late, But Off High On US-China Tension \b0\par\par\uc2 499 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 4 April 2001\par 10:17\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar was higher late Wednesday, but had surrendered most of its early gains following renewed weakness in the yen and Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  amid escalating U.S.-China tensions, dealers said \par \par \uc2  Around 0802 GMT (4:02 a.m. EDT), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.8155, down from S$1.8159 late Tuesday in Asia, but higher than its intraday low below S$1.8100. It traded between S$1.8090 and S$1.8168. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar opened lower in Asia following suspected indirect \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  overnight in London by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Banks and U.S. investors liquidated long U.S. dollar positions in early trade, pushing the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a low of S$1.8090. \par \par \uc2  However, the yen's slide below Y126 to a U.S. dollar and escalating tensions between the U.S. and Chinese governments over the recent midair collision between their military craft prompted renewed weakness in the Singapore dollar and other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the afternoon. \par \par \uc2  Market participants that sold U.S. dollars in the morning were also its main buyers in the afternoon, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar is expected to be supported around S$1.8100 with initial resistance at S$1.8180 and stronger resistance at S$1.8200. \par \par \uc2  Government bonds and interbank money rates moveed in tandem with the Singapore dollar, but trading was quite thin amid a lack of fresh news to trade on. \par \par \uc2  Bond prices initially rose, but ended the day slightly lower than their previous closing level. Money marked rates eased slightly in early trading but inched back to late Tuesday's level in the afternoon. \par \par \uc2  The yield on the 10-year bond ended one basis point higher at 3.68%, and the seven-year bond yield rose one basis point to 3.52%. \par \par \uc2  The yield on five-year government paper rose four basis points to 3.16%, while the two-year bond closed up three basis points at 2.46%. \par \par \uc2  These are pre-settlement closing bids and are subject to minor changes. \par \par \uc2  Historic bond yields and prices can be viewed at the MAS Web site ( https://secure.mas.gov.sg/dataroom/dataroom_findbindex.html ). \par \par \uc2  In the interest rate swaps market, the most liquid seven-year swap offer was at 3.61%, up two basis points from late Tuesday. The 10-year swap offer rose one basis point to 3.81%. \par \par \uc2  The spread between the two- and 10-year swap offers was at 101 basis points compared with 102 basis points Tuesday. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                  Late Wednesday     Late Tuesday\par                  bid      offer     bid     offer\par Overnight        1.8750%  2.0000%   1.5000%  1.7500%\par 3 Month          2.3750%  2.5000%   2.3750%  2.5000%\par 1 Year           2.3750%  2.5000%   2.3750%  2.5000%\par -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; (+65) 421-4809;\par amit.prakash@dowjones.com\par -0- 04/04/01 09-08G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx4401jcg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
