\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  find calm before brewing storm. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Jennifer Chen \par \uc2 693 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 April 2001\par 10:49\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, April 3 (Reuters) - Government rhetoric and a firmer yen gave Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  some respite from their recent losses on Tuesday, but analysts warned the worst was yet to come. \par \par \uc2  Japanese officials expressed concern over the swift decline of the yen to Monday's 2-= year low of 126.84 to the dollar, forcing dollar bulls to retreat for now. \par \par \uc2  Authorities in Seoul and Bangkok also tried to ward off further losses in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  through verbal, and in the case of Korea, actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  However, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar, Korean won, and Thai baht remained mired at lows not seen since 1998, though the Sing dollar did recover from an 11-year low of 1.8235 to the dollar touched in overnight trading. \par \par \uc2  And with the yen expected to resume its slide towards 130 per dollar, Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were expected to again be taken lower. \par \par \uc2  Besides the fragile yen, analysts said the other major bugbear regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were facing was investor risk aversion, which has gripped the market recently. \par \par \uc2  "Risk aversion is bad news for Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  where investors are faster at getting out of positions when things look ugly," said Rebecca Patterson, regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan Chase in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  THE "C" WORDS \par \par \uc2  Since the start of the year, the Sing has shed around four percent of its value, while the won has lost around five percent and the baht over three percent. \par \par \uc2  The rapid fall of regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  has sparked some talk of another \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis. \par \par \uc2  But analysts are quick to stamp out such talk, pointing to important differences between current circumstances and the events that led up to the 1997 Asian crisis. \par \par \uc2  "A lot of these countries basically have current account surpluses, which are narrowing, in contrast to '97/'98, and they've built up their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. Inflation is actually pretty moribund as well," said Mansoor Mohi-uddin, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at UBS Warburg in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  Despite public consternation over the weaker regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the market has begun kicking around the idea that Asia is engaged in "competitive devaluation". \par \par \uc2  "You can call what's going on competitive devaluation. It's not incorrect. The move in the won, peso, baht, and even the Taiwan dollar, they're keeping up with the yen," said JP Morgan Chase's Patterson. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said with the yen headed lower and sentiment towards the region headed the same way, regional central banks were unlikely to spend precious \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves defending their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Artificially propping up a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at a time of regional weakness could also harm export competitiveness. \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE TO REVISE GROWTH \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore said in a quarterly report it would revise down its 5-7 percent growth target for 2001 because of the slowdown in the U.S. and electronics industry, but would not announce the new target until next week. \par \par \uc2  The announcement came against the backdrop of a substantially weaker local dollar and speculation the MAS would switch its current tightening bias to a neutral stance in recognition of deteriorating external factors. \par \par \uc2  Some analysts doubted the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would make such a shift, as it views exchange rate policy as a weapon against imported inflation rather than a tool for export competitiveness. \par \par \uc2  However, others said such a shift had already taken place as the Sing dollar has weakened against its trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , without strong resistance from the MAS. \par \par \uc2  "It's safe to say there is minimal risk that a tighter monetary policy will be pursued as it's completely at odds with fundamentals because the threat is to growth not to inflation," said David Simmonds, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Citibank. \par \par \uc2  While a formal policy shift would be a significant change for the MAS, some analysts doubted whether it would affect the market, given the way the Sing has weakened in the past week. \par \par \uc2  "Even if they come out now and say that they've moving to a neutral policy stance I don't think it will have much of an impact because it's there already in the price," said UBS Warburg's Mohi-uddin. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx4302qop\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
