\par\par \b \uc2  UPDATE 1-Singapore dlr falls past Asian crisis low. \b0\par\par\uc2 505 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 April 2001\par 11:07\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, April 2 (Reuters) - The Singapore dollar buckled under 1.8150 to the U.S. dollar on Monday, falling beyond lows last seen during the Asian financial crisis. \par \par \uc2  It hit a low of 1.8165 in afternoon trade, down 0.7 percent from its late Friday levels of around 1.8030. It has lost more than four percent since the end of last year. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the breach of 1.8150, last hit on January 8, 1998 according to Reuters data, exposed the Singapore dollar to a decline towards decade-lows beyond 1.8300. \par \par \uc2  Its drop on Monday came amid further weakness in the yen and the Thai baht. There was no sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MAS), dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "I don't think they'll (the MAS) come in at this point. Sentiment is just too weak for the Sing dollar," said a chief dealer at a European bank. \par \par \uc2  After falling through 1.8100 psychological support in early trade, the Singapore dollar won a mild respite before extending its decline as European markets opened and the yen revisited 30-month lows around 126.65 to the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the market had grown increasingly bearish amid reduced prospects for the Singapore economy, the fragile yen and euro and anticipation of further merger and acquisition related outflows. \par \par \uc2  But they said the Singapore dollar was by no means isolated in its troubles and the fall owed much to an inevitable spillover effect from other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Some analysts said the Singapore unit's recent fortunes foreshadow tougher times ahead for regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "I think what is going on with the Sing dollar is an illustration of how difficult things will be for the region," said Simon Flint, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of America. \par \par \uc2  "The Sing is an exemplar of an actively managed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and the authorities are clearly concerned about lower global demand." \par \par \uc2  However, Flint pointed out that the weakness in the Singapore dollar had been aggravated by the Singapore Telecommunications Ltd purchase of Australia's Cable & Wireless Optus , which has sparked widespread demand for dollars amid speculation SingTel had yet to fulfil its requirements. \par \par \uc2  Flint said the trade-weighted Sing had actually only fallen by 1.5-2 percent. \par \par \uc2  Chia-Liang Lian, economist at Merrill Lynch in Singapore, said the Sing dollar was being weighed down by corporate demand for U.S. dollars. \par \par \uc2  "We are having a bit of overshooting here due to real corporate demand related to specific purposes as opposed to speculative nature," he said. \par \par \uc2  "But we do expect some pullback (in dollar/Sing) once that corporate demand slows," he said, adding that he was sticking to his mid-2001 forecast of 1.80 for the Sing dollar. \par \par \uc2  The MAS uses the Sing dollar as its main policy tool, managing it against an undisclosed trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Analysts say the basket includes the U.S. dollar, the euro, the yen and other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx4202t78\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
