\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Cower as dollar keeps the upper hand. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,259 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2001\par 10:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, March 30 (Reuters) - Dollar bulls came out in full force late Friday, chasing Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower while the yen and euro flailed. \par \par \uc2  With the end of the Japanese fiscal year around the corner, the yen was flung to 22-month lows in a rumour-wracked market. In late Asian trade, the dollar rose above 125 yen for the first time since October 1998. \par \par \uc2  The euro recovered slightly from 15-week lows amid rumours of possible European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  later in the day. \par \par \uc2  All this cast a pall over already struggling regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Singapore dollar suffered the brunt of the regional weakness, as both the euro and yen make up a substantial chunk of its trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  In North Asia, the won and Taiwan dollar retreated with the yen, prompting verbal and actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by their central banks. Southeast Asian central banks also let the markets know where they stood on the latest bout of regional weakness. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore said late Thursday short-term volatility would not affect its current policy bias towards tightening and the Bank of Thailand said it would let the market determine the baht's level. \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE DOLLAR The Sing dollar lurched lower to 1.8033 in the afternoon as funds and investment houses kept bidding up the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  The latest drop brought its loss to more than four percent since the beginning of the year and put it within striking distance of 1.8150, a level last seen in January 1998. \par \par \uc2  Dealers attributed the fall to the fragile yen and euro, with more weakness expected despite some speculation the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  might have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  late on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  Persistent dollar offers from local banks around the 1.8030 level on Friday fanned speculation they may be acting on behalf of the MAS, after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reiterated on Thursday that it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if needed to curb excessive exchange rate moves. \par \par \uc2  KOREAN WON -2 \par \par \uc2  The won scrambled away from the day's low of 1,331 - a level not seen since October 1998 - as dollar sales by state banks spurred speculation of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the authorities. \par \par \uc2  Dealers in \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  cited state bank dollar sales around the 1,328 won level. Government officials have repeatedly expressed dismay over the won's falls, and threatened to take action in recent days. \par \par \uc2  For the past several weeks, the yen has been the dominant influence on the won's direction as Korea and Japan compete on key exports. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said offshore bidding interest in the dollar on the non-deliverable forward market was fairly strong across the board. At 0800 GMT, the six-month won was at 1,335.8/36.8, and the one-year was at 1,342.8/43.8. \par \par \uc2  On the macroeconomic front, March inflation rose 0.6 percent on month, and 4.4 percent on year, higher than forecasts of 0.3 percent on month and 4.1 percent on year. \par \par \uc2  While the market shrugged off the data, analysts said it had muted hopes for a rate cut when the Bank of Korea holds its next monthly monetary policy meeting on April 6. \par \par \uc2  TAIWAN DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar drooped with the weaker yen and won, but was helped off its intraday low of 32.898 by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rate cut of 12.5 basis points on Thursday also damped sentiment towards the local dollar, though the move was widely expected. \par \par \uc2  The cut brings the bank's discount rate and accomodation with collateral rate to 4.125 percent and 4.5 percent, respectively. \par \par \uc2  Dealers in Singapore said the bank was spotted in the market in early trade, though they added the amount was not significant. \par \par \uc2  On the non-deliverable forward market, dealers said dollar bids were focused in the one-month and three-month rates, which were at 0.070/90 and 0.200 /230, respectively. \par \par \uc2  INDONESIAN RUPIAH \par \par \uc2  Players in Jakarta momentarily forgot troubles at home only to realise the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  woes plaguing the region, driving the rupiah back to the wrong side of 10,400. \par \par \uc2  Dealers in Jakarta said local corporate dollar demand was strong despite the end of the first quarter, as customers were concerned with the general weakness throughout the region. \par \par \uc2  Bank Indonesia sold dollars around 10,410/20, though dealers estimated the amount to have been only around $20 million. \par \par \uc2  On the brighter side, Jakarta issued new guidelines to improve transparency over corporate debt restructuring with the help of key donors such as the World Bank and IMF. \par \par \uc2  The move raised hopes that differences between the IMF and Jakarta could be narrowing, paving the way towards the eventual resumption of the Fund's $5 billion lending programme. \par \par \uc2  THAI BAHT \par \par \uc2  The baht was within a whisker of fresh three-year lows of 45.00 as the yen past 125.00 against the dollar. \par \par \uc2  Bankok dealers said both offshore and onshore players were rushing to buy the U.S. dollar after Thai Prime Minister Thaksin Sinawatra echoed recent comments by officials that the government would not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Shinawatra also said the baht should not fall much further. \par \par \uc2  Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief economist Atchana Waiquamdee said earlier the bank would let the market decide the appropriate level for the baht. \par \par \uc2  Offshore baht funding costs remained firm, with the one-month forward rate at 30/50 satang per dollar against 38/48 late Thursday. \par \par \uc2  PHILIPPINE PESO \par \par \uc2  The peso ended domestic trade at its session low of 49.51 per dollar, weighed by weakness in regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers said corporate dollar demand prevented it rising above 49.27, but dollar profit-taking limited its downside. They said a convincing break of 49.50 would put the dollar on track for further gains towards 49.70. \par \par \uc2  Amid a dearth of fresh local leads, the fiscal position remained a focal point with Finance Secretary Alberto Romulo saying the government would pursue fiscal consolidation to achieve a budget surplus between 2004 and 2006. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, the government said it was targeting a lower budget deficit of 120.6 billion pesos next year, or about 2.8 percent of gross national product, against this year's 145 billion or 3.8 percent of GNP. \par \par \uc2  But analysts remain concerned about its likely success in lowering the deficit given the difficulties of revenue collection in the Philippines. \par \par \uc2  Separately, National Treasurer Sergio Edeza said the Bureau of Treasury hoped to offer 150 billion pesos worth of Treasury bonds to small, individual investors this year to finance the government's budgetary needs. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  0831 GMT \par \par \uc2   Change on the day \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2     Latest bid     Prev Close    Pct Move \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Japan yen     125.14\tab  123.20\tab    -1.55\par  Sing dlr\tab 1.8024\tab  1.7980\tab    -0.24\par  Taiwan dlr    32.837\tab  32.757\tab    -0.24\par  Korean won   1325.00\tab 1318.10\tab    -0.52\par  Baht\tab      44.94\tab   44.67\tab    -0.60\par  Peso\tab      49.45\tab   48.79\tab    -1.33\par  Rupiah\tab 10410.00     10380.00\tab    -0.29\par  Ringgit\tab  3.7995\tab  3.7995\tab    +0.00\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Change on year\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \tab Latest     End prev yr     Pct Move\par  Japan yen     125.14\tab  114.45\tab    -8.54\par  Sing dlr\tab 1.8024\tab  1.7365\tab    -3.66\par  Taiwan dlr    32.837\tab  32.900\tab    +0.19\par  Korean won   1325.00\tab 1277.00\tab    -3.62\par  Baht\tab      44.94\tab   43.70\tab    -2.76\par  Peso\tab      49.45\tab   50.75\tab    +2.63\par  Rupiah\tab 10410.00\tab 9470.00\tab    -9.03\par  Ringgit\tab  3.7995\tab  3.7995\tab    +0.00.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010713dx3u02xq5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
