\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Dn Late; Gives Up Early Gains As Yen Falls \b0\par\par\uc2 619 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2001\par 10:57\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar tracked the yen lower late Friday, surrendering early gains on suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0851 GMT (3:51 a.m. EST), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.8036, up from S$1.7995 late Thursday in Asia. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar opened lower and quickly fell to an intraday low of S$1.7953, on selling by local banks suspected to be acting on MAS' behalf. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said these banks had also capped the U.S. dollar's rise in London overnight after the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decided to leave interest rates unchanged. \par \par \uc2  Late Thursday, the MAS declined to comment on the Singapore dollar's fall to a fresh 38-month low of S$1.8040. However, it repeated its statement issued Monday to Dow Jones Newswires that any short-term volatility in the Singapore dollar "will not" affect its declared, moderately tight monetary policy stance or the undisclosed band within which it manages the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The MAS manages the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate as its primary policy tool, it doesn't set interest rates. \par \par \uc2  However, the U.S. dollar recovered quickly against the local unit on the back of the U.S. unit's rise above Y125. It rose to an intraday high around S$1.8036, but further gains were capped by fears that the MAS or its agent banks would again \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they expect the U.S. dollar to test immediate resistance around S$1.8045-50 near-term if the yen weakens further. \par \par \uc2  A Bank of America research note said that, despite recent suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the MAS may have loosened its monetary policy stance a tad. \par \par \uc2  However, research issued by the government-linked DBS Bank earlier Thursday said the MAS hasn't changed its policy stance to neutral from a moderate tightening bias. \par \par \uc2  DBS, which often acts on behalf of MAS in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, "reminded" investors that Singapore doesn't use a weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to boost exports and that domestic price stability is a "pivotal" policy objective. \par \par \uc2  Singapore imports almost everything it needs, including water. \par \par \uc2  A weaker Singapore dollar also hurt government bonds, which were sold amid light trading. \par \par \uc2  Interbank money market rates edged higher in the wake of a weaker local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Interest rate swaps were also driven higher by weakness in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and bond markets. \par \par \uc2  The yield on the 10-year bond ended three basis points higher at 3.66%, and the seven-year bond yield also rose three basis points to 3.49%. \par \par \uc2  The yield on the five-year government paper rose one basis point to 3.10%. The two-year bond ended at 2.43%, up three basis points. \par \par \uc2  These are pre-settlement closing bids and are subject to minor changes. \par \par \uc2  Historic bond yields and prices can be viewed at the MAS Web site (https://secure.mas.gov.sg/dataroom/dataroom_findbindex.htm l). \par \par \uc2  In the interest rate swaps market, the most liquid seven-year swap offer was at 3.57%, up six basis points from late Thursday. The 10-year swap offer rose seven basis points to 3.79%. \par \par \uc2  The spread between the two- and 10-year swaps was 107 basis points, from 106 basis points Thursday. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                  Late Friday        Late Thursday\par                  bid      offer     bid     offer\par Overnight        1.2500%  1.3750%   1.2500%  1.3750%\par 3 Month          2.3125%  2.4375%   2.2250%  2.3750%\par 1 Year           2.3125%  2.4375%   2.3125%  2.4375%\par -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; (+65) 421-4809;\par amit.prakash@dowjones.com\par -0- 30/03/01 09-50G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx3u01eyw\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
