\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Soft yen hurts, euro drives Sing dlr lower. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,159 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2001\par 11:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, March 29 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  stumbled over the softer yen late Thursday with the Singapore dollar dropped past 1.8000 per dollar, a level not seen since January 1998. \par \par \uc2  With hopes for a European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate cut later Thursday dashed after stronger than expected money supply growth, dealers said players pushed the Sing dollar lower in anticipation of a sell-off in the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The two have been moving in tandem lately because the euro is an important component in the Sing's trade-weighted basket. \par \par \uc2  While other Southeast Asian markets took their cue from the Sing's fall, North Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  drooped with the yen. \par \par \uc2  Given the competition between Japan, Taiwan, and Korea on electronics exports, the Taiwan dollar and won often track the yen. But those correlations have gotten stronger recently, especially in the won. \par \par \uc2  Regional officials were voicing their views of the weakened state of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Thai Finance Minister Somkid Jatusripitak said the government did not mind the weaker baht, while Korean officials expressed dismay over the won's fall. \par \par \uc2  State banks propped up the ailing rupiah, while local banks were spotted buying the Sing, spurring talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The Sing dollar found no solace despite a volatile afternoon session that saw it rebound to 1.7950 from a fresh 38-month low of 1.8040. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said local banks were heavily selling dollar/Sing at levels above 1.8020, sparking speculation they were acting on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore (MAS). \par \par \uc2  "They bought at least $100 million," a European bank trader said. \par \par \uc2  But dealers saw solid demand for the dollar, which supported it at 1.7950. They also said treasurers of mid-sized companies wanted to buy more dollars due to fears of deteriorating hedging costs. \par \par \uc2  An analyst at a U.S. bank forecast that the Sing will test 1.8150, a low hit on January 8, 1998 according to Reuters data, within two weeks. \par \par \uc2  THAI BAHT \par \par \uc2  The baht was bruised after hitting fresh lows at 44.75, its lowest since March 1998, in tandem with the falling Sing. \par \par \uc2  The latest upset was also caused by Finance Minister Somkid Jatusripitak, who said the government may cancel guarantees on foreign bank deposits. \par \par \uc2  Adding insult to the baht's injury, he said he was not concerned about the weak baht and the government would not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the market. \par \par \uc2  He said the weakness of the baht and regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  was caused by the slowdown of the U.S. and Japanese economies. \par \par \uc2  "It just cheered up the (baht) bears," a Bangkok dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Jatusripitak also said he had asked foreign banks in Thailand to increase deposit rates and cut lending rates to help boost the Thai economy. \par \par \uc2  The harried baht failed to even draw much support from firmer baht offshore funding costs. The one-month forward rate rose to 38/48 satang per dollar from 15/18 late on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the baht was not likely to escape the weaker yen and the Sing, and would break 45.00 within a few weeks. \par \par \uc2  INDONESIAN RUPIAH \par \par \uc2  The rupiah was steady after state banks sold about $11 million and corporates waited for parliament's response to President Abdurrahman Wahid's rejection of its censure. \par \par \uc2  "Most of our customers are holding back as they are not sure if the power struggle between the President and Parliament is over or another censure is on its way," said a dealer at a U.S. bank in Jakarta. \par \par \uc2  Wahid's critics said another censure would be issued by parliament, moving closer towards an impeachment vote. \par \par \uc2  Adding to the uncertainty was the IMF's position on whether it would send a review team to Jakarta, a step towards resumption of lending withheld since last December. \par \par \uc2  The IMF's senior Indonesian representative John Dodsworth said he was optimistic a team would arrive next month. Earlier, an IMF spokesman in Washington said it was too early to set a date for sending a mission. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market was waiting for IMF's formal decision on whether to resume lending. \par \par \uc2  PHILIPPINE PESO \par \par \uc2  The peso fell, but was off a session low of 49.45 helped by dollar inflows from exporters and overseas workers. \par \par \uc2  The dollar/peso was also overbought, with a reading of 86 on the 14-day relative strength index. \par \par \uc2  As widely expected, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  left its key overnight rates unchanged at its weekly policy-making meeting. \par \par \uc2  Governor Rafael Buenaventura said the bank would be watching for domestic inflation data and moves by the Federal Reserve. \par \par \uc2  The overnight borrowing rate now stands at 10.0 percent and the lending rate at 12.25 percent. \par \par \uc2  KOREAN WON -2 \par \par \uc2  Verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the authorities failed to help the won, which hit a day's low of 1,319.7. \par \par \uc2  Investors fretted over the yen's outlook, which has been the main force behind the won. Dealers also said the market was disappointed by lacklustre month-end dollar sales by exporters. \par \par \uc2  A Bank of Korea official said the won's "instability" could affect the economy, adding the bank was monitoring the market. \par \par \uc2  Later, a Finance Ministry official echoed the warning, saying the government was worried about the won's movements. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said the market, while cautious, was more likely to react to these comments if actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  followed. \par \par \uc2  February industrial output rose slightly more than expected, expanding 0.6 percent on month and 8.6 percent on year. The data had little impact on the market. \par \par \uc2  TAIWAN DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar retreated with the yen and won, with the market focusing on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's monetary board meeting. \par \par \uc2  Gains on the local bourse failed to give the local dollar much of lift, as investors remained more concerned about the yen's direction. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was widely expected to cut rates by 12.5 basis points, bringing its discount rate and accommodation with collateral rates to 4.25 percent and 4.625 percent, respectively. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  0912 GMT \par \par \uc2   Change on the day \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2     Latest bid     Prev Close    Pct Move \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Japan yen     123.37\tab  121.74\tab    -1.32\par  Sing dlr\tab 1.7990\tab  1.7927\tab    -0.35\par  Taiwan dlr     32.76\tab   32.67\tab    -0.27\par  Korean won   1318.10\tab 1304.60\tab    -1.02\par  Baht\tab      44.72\tab   44.36\tab    -0.81\par  Peso\tab      48.79\tab   49.15\tab    +0.74\par  Rupiah\tab 10400.00     10365.00\tab    -0.34\par  Ringgit\tab  3