\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  ASIA:Singapore Dlr Hits New Lows Amid MAS Easing Talk \b0\par\par\uc2  By Amit Prakash \par \uc2 946 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2001\par 10:21\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    A Dow Jones Newswires Column\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar plunged to another\par 38-month low Thursday without opposition from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , fueling\par speculation that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore may have subtly\par eased its monetary policy stance from moderately tight to neutral.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar crested psychological resistance at S$1.8000, hitting a high of S$1.8040, as market participants tested the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's resolve to carry out its oft-repeated threat to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves become too volatile. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar hit an all-time low of S$1.8170 in January 1998. \par \par \uc2  If indeed the MAS has changed track, it would amount to a reversal of its standing policy that it expects the local dollar to reflect the Singapore economy's fundamentals, which remain strong. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reiterated that stance as recently as Monday. \par \par \uc2  However, the Singapore dollar recovered by late afternoon, as jittery bankers and investors sold U.S. dollars amid \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears. Light U.S. dollar selling by two local banks also aided the Singapore dollar's recovery, but the selling wasn't aggressive enough to suggest that it was at the MAS' behest, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  At 0740 GMT, the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7998. \par \par \uc2  Analysts and dealers say that the MAS' reluctance to defend the local dollar even though its recent decline has outpaced that of the yen points to a rethinking of its declared policy of facilitating a gradual and modest appreciation of the trade-weighted \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The MAS manages the Singapore dollar exchange rate against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the country's major trading partners and competitors. It maintains the trade-weighted exchange rate within an undisclosed band. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar has "been out of the band for about two weeks now," says Rebecca Patterson, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with J.P. Morgan in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  "It the MAS sees the move caused by things outside its control, and because Singapore's growth is also weakening, it could be that it's moving towards a more neutral policy stance," she adds. \par \par \uc2  In fact, Patterson says that despite the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's "rhetoric" about a strong local dollar, the MAS has remained neutral this year. This likely explains the Singapore dollar's descent from S$1.7318 at the beginning of this year. \par \par \uc2  Notions that the MAS might have changed its tune - at least for the time being - were supported Thursday by research published by government-linked DBS Bank Ltd. In a research note, DBS said that the Singapore dollar's decline suggests a "more flexible" MAS policy may be in play in view of low inflation and slowing growth in exports. \par \par \uc2  Inflation eased to 1.3% on year in February from 2% in January. \par \par \uc2  After having expanded at a rapid 9.9% last year, Singapore's economy is expected to grow at a slower pace this year. Analysts say growth could come at the lower end or below the government's 5% to 7% forecast range, as a slowing U.S. economy and global electronics downturn take their toll on exports. \par \par \uc2  Analysts at Citibank/Salomon Smith Barney and Bank of America said they expect the MAS to formally switch to a neutral policy stance within the next three to six months. \par \par \uc2  Though a comment from the MAS wasn't immediately available, the head of research at DBS Bank cautioned against interpreting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's current flexible stance as a policy change. \par \par \uc2  "The MAS has always said that it is not in Singapore's long term interest to have a weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to compete globally," said Fong Cheng Hong. \par \par \uc2  "We (Singapore) are not China. We don't have a huge resource base. So our companies need to increase productivity and move into high end products with high margins to stay ahead. Also, we are heavy importers, so a stronger Singapore dollar will always be in interest of our long term stability," she said, adding that "I think this (outlook) has not changed." \par \par \uc2  Fong attributes the local dollar's recent slide to a weaker euro and merger and acquisition outflow related to Singapore Telecommunication Ltd.'s (P.SGT) successful bid for Australia's Cable and Wireless Optus (A.CWO) valued at up to A$17.2 billion. The euro has slipped from US$0.9298 on March 1 to around US$0.8866 currently. \par \par \uc2  MAS' reluctance to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says Fong, reflects its pragmatism as \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would likely be an unwise move in the face of strong market forces. \par \par \uc2  "If you watch the MAS (actions) over the past 10 years, you will see that they come in only when conditions are right, says Fong. \par \par \uc2  This means that speculators need to watch out for the MAS lurking in the wings once the euro and yen show signs of recovery. Fong expects the Singapore dollar to recover to S$1.77-S$178 by end-June and around S$1.72-S$1.75 by end December. \par \par \uc2  While most analysts expect the Singapore dollar to recover by the end of the year if the local economy's strong fundamentals kick in on the back of a U.S. recovery, some also believe that the Singapore dollar has yet to hit a bottom. \par \par \uc2  Recent research issued by Bank of America and ABN Amro Bank forecast the Singapore dollar will slide to S$1.82 by June, a level not seen since June 1990. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; (+65) 421-4809;\par amit.prakash@dowjones.com\par -0- 29/03/01 09-29G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  4258 5095 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx3t01dio\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
