\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Dn Late But Off New 38-Mo Low; MAS Abstains \b0\par\par\uc2 632 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2001\par 11:11\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar was lower late Wednesday, but off a new 38-month low as market participants sold U.S. dollars amid fears that the island's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Around 0855 GMT (3.55 a.m. EST), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.7933, up from S$1.7930 late Tuesday in Asia. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar opened lower, tracking its overnight decline below Y122, but rose steadily against the Singapore dollar on stop-loss buying, as the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  once again abstained from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "People had been expecting some sort of smoothening or (damage) control by the MAS, but we saw stop-losses being triggered when they failed to show up," said a dealer at a U.S. brokerage. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the U.S. dollar rose to a new 38-month high of S$1.7958, with one dealer saying that a few direct interbank deals were likely done at S$1.7961. \par \par \uc2  These are the highest traded levels for the U.S. dollar since it rose to levels around S$1.8140 in January 1998. \par \par \uc2  In an afternoon research note, Bank of America said that even though "there's not been a sniff by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far," it favors a short U.S. dollar position through Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The bank advised investors to be prepared to "junk" that short position Friday given the risk of a weaker yen and rupiah in the medium term, and its expectation that the MAS would loosen monetary policy over the next three to six months. \par \par \uc2  "Hence, our S$1.82 call within the coming three months holds, and the main risk (for the U.S. dollar) is to the upside," it said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Meanwhile, dealers said that they expect the U.S. dollar to face\par strong psychological resistance at S$1.80 near term, with support around\par S$1.79.\par - - 28/03/01 09-11G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On the bond market, prices of Singapore Government Securities ended\par lower, tracking an overnight decline in U.S. Treasuries and due to\par renewed weakness in the Singapore dollar, dealers said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Interbank money market rates, however, were mostly steady amid flush\par liquidity in the banking system.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Interest rate swaps mostly tracked bond yields higher, but came off\par their intraday highs as the Singapore dollar bounced off a new 38-month\par low.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The yield on the 10-year bond ended three basis points higher at 3.61%\par and the seven-year bond yield also rose three basis points to 3.44%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The yield on five-year government paper rose one basis point to 3.04%.\par The two-year bond ended at 2.40%, up four basis points from the average\par yield at Tuesday's auction of S$260 billion worth of two-year bonds.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par These are pre-settlement closing bids and are subject to minor\par changes. Historic bond yields and prices can be viewed at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary\par Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Singapore's Web site\par (https://secure.mas.gov.sg/dataroom/dataroom_findbindex.htm l).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the interest rate swaps market, the most liquid seven-year swap\par offer was at 3.51%, up three basis points from late Tuesday. The 10-year\par swap offer was flat at 3.70%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The spread between the two- and 10-year swaps was unchanged at 105\par basis points.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                  Late Wednesday     Late Tuesday\par                  bid      offer     bid     offer\par Overnight        1.2500%  1.5000%   1.2500%  1.5000%\par 3 Month          2.1250%  2.2500%   2.1250%  2.2500%\par 1 Year           2.2500%  2.3750%   2.1875%  2.3750%\par -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; (+65) 421-4809;\par amit.prakash@dowjones.com\par -0- 28/03/01 09-34G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx3s01ezg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
