\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Up Sharply Vs USD; US Banks Sold US Dlrs \b0\par\par\uc2 364 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2001\par 03:18\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par - - 26/03/01 01-18G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0118GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Heavy selling of U.S. dollars by U.S. banks helped the Singapore dollar rebound sharply from a 38-month low early Monday against its U.S. counterpart. \par \par \uc2  Traders say the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore, the island-state's de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , wasn't responsible for the rise in the local dollar. Analysts expect the MAS is poised to curb the slide in the Singapore dollar for fear it will spark inflationary pressures. \par \par \uc2  At 0130 GMT (8:30 p.m. Sunday EST), the U.S. dollar was S$1.7890, down from an early high of S$1.7939, a level not traded since January 1998. \par \par \uc2  A chief dealer at a European bank in Singapore said U.S. institutions likely sold U.S. dollars to take profit amid speculation the MAS might step in, and on a "buy the rumor, sell the fact" strategy in relation to Singapore Telecommunications Ltd.'s (P.SGT) takeover bid for Australian-based Cable & Wireless Optus Ltd. (A.CWO). \par \par \uc2  The bid, which was launched Monday, values Australia's second largest telecommunications company at up to A$17 billion, and might require Singtel to exchange local dollars for foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The trader said there are "no signs" of local banks selling U.S. dollars that would normally be associated with MAS action. \par \par \uc2  MAS indicated recently it would provide foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  liquidity to prevent any volatility in the Singapore dollar in the event of any one-time capital flows arising from mergers and acquisitions. \par \par \uc2  This along with a report issued last week by \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 's DBS Bank, which said MAS might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  around S$1.7900 unless the U.S. dollar breaks above Y125, is fueling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears. \par \par \uc2  DBS is one of a number of local banks that traders say sometimes act on behalf of the MAS. \par \par \uc2  The European trader expects the U.S. dollar will find support at S$1.7850, and meet resistance at S$1.7950 and then at S$1.8000. \par \par \uc2  -By Benjamin Pedley, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-421-4485; ben.pedley@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx3q01brn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
