\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Sets New 31-Mo Low As USD Advances In Asia \b0\par\par\uc2 646 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2001\par 09:14\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar fell to a new 31-month low late Thursday as the U.S. dollar continued to advance against most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Around 0812 GMT (3:12 a.m. EST), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.7870, up from S$1.7810 late Wednesday in Asia, and higher than its previous 31-month high of S$1.7850 reached overnight. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the U.S. dollar gained on broad-based buying, but its gains against the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  were capped by fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "I'm very hesitant to buy at these levels because the dollar is looking overbought and I'm concerned about MAS \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," a dealer at a European bank said. \par \par \uc2  Earlier Thursday, two local banks offered to sell U.S. dollars around S$1.7845-S$1.7850, but the offers were unlikely to have been made at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's behest, a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  "The market's long (and) it would have been vulnerable to a selloff if it had been the MAS," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  In a research note, Singapore's DBS Bank said the U.S dollar was trading near the upper limit of MAS' undisclosed policy band, and that U.S. dollar bulls should note there's scope for a "substantial downside correction." \par \par \uc2  The MAS manages the Singapore dollar exchange rate against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the country's major trading partners and competitors. It maintains the trade-weighted exchange rate within an undisclosed band. \par \par \uc2  DBS estimates that the MAS band currently ranges between S$1.7610 and S$1.7880. \par \par \uc2  Unless the U.S. dollar firmly breaches 125 Japanese yen, market participants shouldn't expect it to break above S$1.79 as the MAS may likely \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  around that region, DBS added. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dealers said they expect the U.S. dollar to trade in a\par S$1.7800-S$1.7880 range overnight.\par - - 22/03/01 08-14G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The U.S. dollar subsequently briefly rose to an intraday high of\par S$1.7905, its highest level since hitting S$1.8010 on Jan. 13, 1998,\par dealers said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par However, it quickly retreated on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  fears and was quoted at\par S$1.7878 at 0920 GMT (4:20 a.m. EST).\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par A weaker Singapore dollar pushed interbank money rates higher, while\par bonds fell sharply amid jitters in the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, market\par participants said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Interest rate swaps tracked bond yields higher, and continued to rise\par after the bond market closed, on a weaker Singapore dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dealers said if interest rate swaps open around current levels Friday,\par then bond prices would likely open lower.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The yield on the 10-year bond ended nine basis points higher at 3.68%\par and the seven-year bond yield also rose nine basis points to 3.51%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The yield on five-year government paper rose eight basis points to\par 3.11% and the two-year bond yield rose 13 basis points to 2.36%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par These are pre-settlement closing bids and are subject to minor\par changes.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the interest rate swaps market, the most liquid seven-year swap\par offer was at 3.61%, up eight basis points from late Wednesday. The\par 10-year swap offer was up 10 basis points at 3.81%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The spread between the two- and 10-year swaps narrowed to 105 basis\par points from 113, resulting in a flatter swap curve.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                  Late Thursday      Late Wednesday\par                  bid      offer     bid     offer\par Overnight        1.2500%  1.5000%   1.2500%  1.3750%\par 3 Month          2.3750%  2.5000%   2.2500%  2.3750%\par 1 Year           2.3750%  2.5000%   2.1875%  2.3125%\par -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; (+65) 421-4809;\par amit.prakash@dowjones.com\par -0- 22/03/01 09-29G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx3m019pk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
