\par\par \b \uc2  US Dollar Hits S$1.79; First Time Since Jan 1998 \b0\par\par\uc2 144 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2001\par 09:38\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The U.S. dollar briefly rose above 1.79 Singapore dollars for the first time since January 1998, dealers said Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar rose to an intraday high of S$1.7905, a dealer at a European bank said. \par \par \uc2  However, the U.S. dollar quickly retreated amid fears that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and was quoted at S$1.7878 at 0851 GMT (3:51 a.m. EST). \par \par \uc2  According to Bridge Telerate data, the last time the U.S. dollar traded above S$1.79 was on Jan. 13, 1998, when it an intraday high of S$1.8010. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; (+65) 421-4809;\par amit.prakash@dowjones.com\par -0- 22/03/01 08-53G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx3m019rf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
