\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Weak Yen Holds Singapore Dollar To Ransom \b0\par\par\uc2  By Amit Prakash \par \uc2 746 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2001\par 08:30\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    A Dow Jones Newswires Column\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  contagion pushed the Singapore dollar\par to its lowest level since September 1998 Friday, as U.S. dollar bulls\par continued to gore the euro, yen and other Asian units.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  With the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore appearing reluctant to defend it against such strong negative forces, in coming weeks the Singapore dollar will likely continue to be held to ransom by a weak yen, in particular, as the Bank of Japan grapples with its policy options to help avert a recession. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar overnight fell below the psychologically important level of S$1.77 against the U.S. dollar as the yen plummeted below Y122, for the first time since July 1999, on speculation that the U.S. wouldn't resist a weaker yen to stimulate growth in Japan. The euro fell below S$0.90 for the first time since December. \par \par \uc2  After a brief recovery from its early morning low of S$1.7720, the Singapore dollar fell to an intraday low of S$1.7740 at 0712 GMT as banks and companies resumed buying U.S. dollars. This is the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's lowest level since hitting S$1.7785 on Sept. 3, 1998. \par \par \uc2  A weak Thai baht and Indonesian rupiah - which initially held up around IDR10,000 before falling below IDR10,350, - also contributed to the Singapore dollar's fall. \par \par \uc2  Bank of America \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Simon Flint said that as long as the yen continues to weaken, the Singapore dollar will drift lower, punctuated by brief period of respite, and "we'll see plenty of (U.S. dollar) buying on dips." \par \par \uc2  At the same time, DBS Bank researchers noted that while the Singapore dollar will stay weak, there also scope for a "substantial" recovery if the negative sentiment toward the yen abates. \par \par \uc2  At home, too, there is little news to cheer the Singapore dollar as large government-linked companies eye overseas acquisitions and the domestic economy slows in line with the downturn in the U.S. economy and global electronics demand. \par \par \uc2  On Thursday, Singapore's Deputy Prime Minister Lee Hsien Loong said that while the government is, "for now", maintaining its 2001 economic growth forecast at 5% to 7%, it is closely monitoring global and regional economic developments that have "clouded" the island's growth prospects. \par \par \uc2  "If the anticipated V-shaped recovery does not come about (in the U.S.), there will be rougher times ahead for Singapore and the rest of the world," Lee said, adding that "We also need to watch the global electronics downturn." \par \par \uc2  In a sign of a rapidly slowing Singapore economy, data released late Thursday sowed that January retail sales rose by a dismal 3.8% on-year, lower than analysts forecasts of a 15% to 18% rise, sharply below 19.3% growth in December. The data indicated that slowing consumer spending may knock the government's hope that strong domestic demand will help offset slowing external demand. \par \par \uc2  Given the current external and domestic situation, the island's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will most likely keep \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on hold, especially since the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar is relatively stronger on a trade weighted basis, Bank Of America's Flint said. \par \par \uc2  The MAS manages the Singapore dollar exchange rate against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the country's major trading partners and competitors. It maintains the trade-weighted exchange rate within an undisclosed band. \par \par \uc2  According to a DBS Bank research note, the Singapore dollar has likely appreciated 0.4% year-to-date on a trade-weighted basis (or compared with \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of major trade partners) while having depreciated 2.2% against the U.S. dollar on the spot market. \par \par \uc2  "The point we are trying to get across is that the MAS' policy of a strong (trade weighted) \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't mean that the Singapore dollar must rise in nominal (or spot) terms," said Fong Cheng Hong, head of research at DBS Bank. \par \par \uc2  According to DBS estimates, the U.S. dollar's recent surge has pushed up MAS' policy band to S$1.7540-S$1.7810 from S$1.7310-S$1.7570 at the start of the year. \par \par \uc2  DBS says the policy band can shift to a higher S$1.7590-S$1.7860 range if the yen and euro continue to plunge. \par \par \uc2  -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; (+65) 421-4809; amit.prakash@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  4258 5095 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx3g015bq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
