\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dollar Higher Late, Tracks Regional  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 544 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2001\par 10:54\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar was higher late Tuesday in Asia as it tracked Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and as banks sold U.S. dollars to lock in profits from the U.S. unit's recent gains, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0919 GMT (4:19 a.m. EST Tuesday), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.7594, down from S$1.7617 late Monday in Asia. Against the yen and the baht it was at Y120.17 and THB43.57 respectively, lower than intraday highs of Y120.62 and THB43.80. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar opened higher, hitting an intraday high of S$1.7634 in early trades, as financial markets were jittery following a sharp overnight fall in the Nasdaq Composite Index in the U.S. \par \par \uc2  However, the U.S. dollar eased from its intraday high, following its decline against the yen and the baht. In the afternoon it fell below S$1.76, on profit-taking led by a large local bank, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers noted that contrary to market expectations, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  following the U.S. dollar's break above S$1.7630. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dealers expect the U.S. dollar to trade in a S$1.7580-S$1.7630 range\par in London and New York later in the day.\par - - 13/03/01 09-54G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The volatility in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market spilled into the interbank money\par markets and the bond market, all of which gyrated in step with the\par Singapore dollar.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Jitters in Singapore's financial market in the early session sent the\par overnight interbank offered rate soaring to 4.75%, its highest level\par this year. However a stronger local dollar in late trading brought the\par overnight interbank rate down to 2.75%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Term rates also eased from late Monday's levels.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Government bond prices shook off sharp early losses to end the day\par higher, boosted by softer interest rates, dealers said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par After mirroring moves in bond yields for most of the day, interest\par rate swaps diverged in the afternoon and were higher compared with late\par Monday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The 10-year bond yield fell two basis points to 3.76% and the\par seven-year yield ended one basis point lower at 3.61%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The yield on five-year government paper fell one basis point to 3.22%.\par The two-year bond yield ended eight basis points lower at 2.29%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par These are pre-settlement closing bids and are subject to minor change.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the interest rate swaps market, the most liquid seven-year swap\par offer was at 3.66%, down from 3.64% late Monday, while the 10-year swap\par offer was at 3.91% up from 3.89%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The spread between the two- and 10-year swaps was at 125 basis points\par lower than 126 late Monday, resulting in a marginally flatter swap\par curve.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                    Late Tuesday      Late Monday\par                  bid     offer     bid     offer\par Overnight       2.5000%  2.7500%   3.7500%  4.0000%\par 3 Month         2.2500%  2.3750%   2.3125%  2.4375%\par 1 Year          2.2500%  2.3750%   2.3125%  2.4375%\par -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; (+65) 421-4809;\par amit.prakash@dowjones.com\par -0- 13/03/01 10-2