\par\par \b \uc2  US Dollar Above S$1.76; First Time Since Nov 27 \b0\par\par\uc2 469 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2001\par 04:58\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par - - 12/03/01 03-58G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par 0358GMT\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The U.S. dollar rose above S$1.76 Monday - the first time since Nov. 27 when it hit a high of S$1.7608, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar's gains against the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  follows its rise above 11,000 Indonesian rupiah and Y120. \par \par \uc2  "Everybody is buying (U.S.) dollars and I don't rule out reaching S$1.77 later today and S$1.78 near term," a dealer at a European bank in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  At 0415 GMT (11:15 p.m. EST Sunday), the U.S. dollar was at S$1.7616 - its highest level since reaching S$1.7785 on Sept. 9, 1998. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said there were no signs of either the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore or local banks selling U.S. dollars to stem the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decline. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said that U.S. dollar's 13% rise against the rupiah Monday and the possibility that it may rise above Y121 later in the day are the likely reasons why the MAS has so far refrained from defending the local dollar. \par \par \uc2  Last week, the MAS said it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth out volatility in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar, particularly if the weakness arises from merger and acquisition outflows, such as Singapore Telecommunications Ltd.'s (P.SGT) A$17.5 billion bid for Australia's Cable and Wireless Optus (A.CWO). \par \par \uc2  A DBS Bank research note, issued earlier Monday, provides possible clues for lack of any MAS action so far to defend the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar. DBS said that any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will help curb short-term volatility, "but won't preclude a further weakening of the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  The government-linked bank, which is also Singapore's largest, said that with weak economic data expected in the first half of this year, the Singapore dollar's value will largely be determined by the regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  situation and U.S. dollar demand from large Singapore companies seeking to acquire overseas assets. \par \par \uc2  The European bank dealer said that if the U.S. dollar succeeded in staying above S$1.7620 during the remainder of Asian trading Monday, it could break the S$1.7650 resistance level and possibly test S$1.77 during London trading. \par \par \uc2  "We expect a surge in buying when London comes in later," he said. \par \par \uc2  A break above S$1.77 could, in the near-term, propel the U.S. dollar to S$1.78 - a level not seen since August 1998. \par \par \uc2  At 0529 GMT (12:59 a.m. EST), the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7635. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; (+65) 421-4809;\par amit.prakashdowjones.com\par -0- 12/03/01 05-43G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx3c013o3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
