\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Up Late, But Expected To Weaken On IDR, M&A \b0\par\par\uc2 637 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2001\par 08:53\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar was higher late Friday, tracking a stronger yen, but was off its intraday high due to a weaker Indonesian rupiah and U.S. dollar buying for merger and acquisition activities, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0741 GMT (2:41 a.m. EST), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.7571, down from S$1.7588 late Thursday in Asia. Against the yen, it was at Y119.51 compared with its intraday high of Y119.88. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar fell in early trading, hitting an intraday low of S$1.7543, on profit-taking led by a local banks. \par \par \uc2  However, in the afternoon, worsening political uncertainly in Indonesia pushed the U.S. dollar above the key 10,000 rupiah level. This helped it to regain some lost ground against the Singapore dollar. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar's recovery from its intraday low was also aided by buying likely related to government-linked Singapore Telecommunications Ltd.'s bid for Australia's Cable & Wireless Optus, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "The dollar/sing is looking quite well bid and I won't be surprised if it tests S$1.7600 tonight because no one wants to go short into the weekend, especially with the rupiah tanking," a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  However, market participants will be cautious in bidding up the U.S. dollar after the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 's said earlier this week that it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth out \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  volatility arising from merger and acquisition related activities, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar is expected to be well supported at S$1.7550 with initial resistance at S$1.7580 and then around S$1.7600-S$1.7620. \par \par \uc2  Action in the bond and swaps market was largely driven by money market rates, which initially rose on tight liquidity before easing in the afternoon in line with a stronger Singapore dollar. \par \par \uc2  Prices of Singapore Government Securities ended slightly lower, but off their early lows. \par \par \uc2  Prices fell in the morning on tight money market liquidity and also because players adjusted their portfolios ahead of an expected slew of higher yielding corporate issuance, given that Singapore dollar debt is among cheapest in Asia, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Thursday's cut in the minimum bid price for Singapore's third-generation mobile phone licenses is also expected to result in successful bidders issuing bonds to fund the 3G network rollout, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  However, in the afternoon, bonds were bought back after the overnight interbank offered rate eased from its intraday high of 4%, its highest level this year. Term rates also eased slightly. \par \par \uc2  Interest rate swaps largely tracked movements in bond yields, and were higher than Thursday's levels. \par \par \uc2  The 10-year bond yield was one basis point higher at 3.67% in late trade and the seven-year yield also rose one basis point to 3.52%. \par \par \uc2  The yield on the five-year government paper rose one basis point to 3.09% while the two-year bond yield was down one basis point at 2.19%. \par \par \uc2  In the interest rate swaps market, the most liquid seven-year swap was at 3.55%, compared with 3.54% late Thursday, while the 10-year swap was at 3.79% compared with 3.78%. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The spread between the two-and 10-year swaps was at 127 basis points\par compared with 126 late Thursday, resulting in a marginally steeper swap\par curve.\par                    Late Friday       Late Thursday\par                  bid     offer 