\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  - Clouded by yen, outlook bearish. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,237 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2001\par 10:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, March 8 (Reuters) - The beleaguered yen towered over Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in late trade on Thursday, prompting more analysts to revise down their near-term regional forecasts. \par \par \uc2  The yen remained weak but was off fresh 20-month lows hit earlier in the day after Finance Minister Kiichi Miyazawa said Japan's fiscal condition was on the brink of collapse. \par \par \uc2  Although it managed to poked above 120 in late trade and lifted most \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from their intraday lows, overall sentiment remained bearish. "A weak yen policy would trigger a new round of competitive depreciation, given the high dependence on external demand and electronics in the region," said Gerard Teo, economist at ABN AMRO Bank. \par \par \uc2  "We have modestly adjusted the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  forecast profiles for some regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  given the weaker yen," Teo said in a report issued on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  ABN AMRO lowered its end-March forecast for the Korean won to 1,280 from 1,250 previously, and for the Taiwan dollar to 32.50 from 32.00. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah slid to 9,915 - its worst level since October 7, 1998 - as robust corporate demand for the dollar outweighed offers by state banks. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso was briefly jolted by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's 50-basis-point rate cut, but managed to stand above the 48 level. \par \par \uc2  PHILIPPINE PESO \par \par \uc2  The peso ended virtually unchanged from Wednesday's close, but sentiment towards the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was fraying. \par \par \uc2  Dealers in Manila said it dipped in line with the yen and the Thai baht, adding there was still a perception in the market of the similarities between the Thai and Philippine economies. \par \par \uc2  While many dealers were still expecting the peso to appreciate due to the better political climate, they said the bearish sentiment towards regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  was catching. "The peso was starting from a weak position, anyway," said a dealer at a European bank in Manila. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to cut rates did surprise some in the market. While the bank was widely expected to ease rates in the future given the slowing economy, some players were expecting the rate cut after the Federal Reserve's meeting on March 20. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut its key overnight borrowing and lending rates by 50 basis points, bringing them to 10.5 percent and 12.75 percent respectively, effective from Monday. \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The Sing dollar revisited overnight lows around 1.7595 to the U.S. dollar on broad-based selling in light of the yen's fall. \par \par \uc2  The yen is believed to make up between 10 and 15 percent of the trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  against which the Singapore dollar is managed. Dealers said a breach of 1.7595, which the Sing dollar has touched thrice but not broken since November 27, could expose it to further losses towards its late November lows at 1.7615/20. \par \par \uc2  But that level was expected to be a firm support unless the yen weakened radically as the Sing dollar has not slipped beyond that point since September 1998. ABN AMRO left its end-March forecast for the Sing dollar unchanged at 1.75, but revised its end-June forecast to 1.76 from 1.74 previously in light of the weaker yen. \par \par \uc2  KOREAN WON -2 \par \par \uc2  The won was lower after another session spent tracking the yen's fading fortunes. \par \par \uc2  Korea and Japan compete on a number of exports, especially those headed for the U.S., which makes the won beholden to the yen's movements. \par \par \uc2  However, despite the yen's breach of 120, the won's losses were capped in a market already heavily long of dollars. Dollar profit-taking helped the won off its day low of 1,279.0. \par \par \uc2  Chartists said the dollar was bumping against the 70 mark on the 14-day relative strength index, easing slightly from an over 70 reading earlier in the day. \par \par \uc2  The market took in stride the Bank of Korea's decision to hold its key overnight call rate steady at five percent for March. The move was widely anticipated, though analysts said they expect further rate cuts given Korea's slowing economy. TAIWAN DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar fell back slightly on the back of the yen's losses, but stayed above the 32.40 mark thanks to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers in \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the bank was spotted in the market selling U.S. dollars when the local dollar slipped past 32.40. A dealer at a U.S. bank in Singapore estimated the bank spent around $100 million in its defence on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  ABN AMRO cited the weaker yen, less aggressive easing by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  than originally expected and slowing foreign fund inflows as reasons for its lowered Taiwan dollar forecasts. \par \par \uc2  INDONESIAN RUPIAH \par \par \uc2  The rupiah hit its lowest level since October 7, 1998 as political and economic gloom spurred corporate demand for the dollar which outpaced offers from state banks. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the country's mountain of debt, social unrest, problems at the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , political disunity, and deepening conflict with the IMF continued to weigh on sentiment. \par \par \uc2  On the macro front, Salomon Smith Barney said Indonesia's weak imports of capital goods and some raw materials signalled a poor economic outlook. \par \par \uc2  "Recent depreciation of the rupiah has not helped exports to rebound, but has hurt imports," it said in a report. \par \par \uc2  But it expected inflation to creep up in March and April on the back of bad weather conditions, the government's imposition of sales tax on luxury goods, and upcoming adjustments to domestic fuel prices. \par \par \uc2  THAI BAHT \par \par \uc2  The baht crawled above 43.60 as high baht funding costs offshore forced some players to unwind long dollar positions. \par \par \uc2  The one-month offshore forward rate rose to 5/7 satang per dollar earlier before easing to 3/6 satang on the back of dollar offers. Onshore rate of the same tenor was slightly firmer at -10.25/-9.75 satang. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said the baht was unlikely to firm beyond 43.40 this week given keen demand to buy the dollar on dips. \par \par \uc2  On the political front, Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra said on Thursday he knew who was behind the bombing of a Thai Airways plane he had been due to board, but the mastermind might never be caught due to a lack of evidence. \par \par \uc2  News of the explosion dampened baht sentiment earlier this week as some foreign investors believed the incident raised Thai political risk. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  0926 GMT \par \par \uc2   Change on the day \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2     Latest bid     Prev Close    Pct Move \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Japan yen     119.51\tab  119.29\tab    -0.18\par  Sing dlr\tab 1.7565\tab  1.7550\tab    -0.09\par  Taiwan dlr    32.381\tab  32.370\tab    -0.03\par  Korean won   127