\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Dn Late, Tracking Yen Weakness \b0\par\par\uc2 525 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2001\par 08:53\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar continued to weaken against the dollar late Thursday on the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength across the region, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0750 GMT (2:50 a.m. EST), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.7588, up from S$1.7571 late Wednesday in Asia. It traded in a S$1.7565-S$1.7598 range Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar's weakness tracked the losses in the value of the yen against the U.S. dollar. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  continued to trade above Y120, but retreated in late trading. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar is expected to remain bid in the wake of a weak yen and on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  outflows to fund acquisitions by large Singapore companies. \par \par \uc2  However, the U.S. dollar isn't likely to be bid sharply higher following a recent statement by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  that it will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to smooth out volatility arising from merger and acquisition-related outflows. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dealers said they expect the U.S. dollar to trade in a\par S$1.7550-$1.7595 range overnight.\par - - 08/03/01 07-53G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On the bond market, prices of Singapore Government Securities\par surrendered early gains and ended sharply lower.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Bonds opened higher in line with overnight gains in U.S. Treasurys,\par but fell sharply in midmorning and afternoon trades.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The midmorning selling was driven by interest rate swaps, resulting\par from sudden buying in the usually illiquid 10-year swaps. Buying a swap\par usually creates a short position in the bond market, and vice versa.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the afternoon, profit-taking on bonds automatically pushed interest\par rate swaps higher.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "Bond prices have risen quite sharply this year, and players don't see\par much scope for them to rise further in the near term. So they decided to\par sell," a primary dealer said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Higher interbank interest rate, resulting from a weaker Singapore\par dollar this week, also encouraged bond selling, dealers said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The 10-year bond yield ended seven basis points higher at 3.67% and\par the seven-year yield also rose seven basis points to 3.51%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The yield on the five-year government paper rose six basis points to\par 3.08% while the two-year bond yield was up nine basis points at 2.21%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par These bond yields are pre-settlement bids quoted by primary dealers.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In the interest rate swaps market, the most liquid seven-year swap was\par at 3.54%, compared with 3.51% late Wednesday, while the 10-year swap was\par at 3.78% compared with 3.74%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The spread between the two-and 10-year swaps was at 126 basis points\par compared with 125 late Wednesday, resulting in a marginally steeper swap\par curve.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                    Late Thursday     Late Wednesday\par                  bid     offer     bid     offer\par Overnight       3.2500%  3.3750%   2.5000%  2.7500%\par 3 Month         2.0625%  2.1875%   1.8750%  2.0000%\par 1 Year          2.1875%  2.3125%   2.1250%  2.2500%\par -By Hasan Jafri and Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; (+65) 421-4809;\par amit.prakash@dowjones.