\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Lower In Late Trade; Mirrors Weaker Yen \b0\par\par\uc2 459 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2001\par 10:27\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar was lower against its U.S counterpart late Wednesday, mirroring a weaker Japanese yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0907 GMT (4:07 a.m. EST), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.7571, up from S$1.7536 late Monday in Asia. It traded in a S$1.7544-S$1.7572 range Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Singapore's markets were closed Tuesday in observance of the Hari Raya Haji, a Muslim festival. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar opened higher but fell in opening trades on selling by a large local bank. But it soon recovered and rose in line with its gains against the yen, dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar is expected to remain bid in the wake of a weak yen and on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  outflows to fund acquisitions by large Singapore companies. \par \par \uc2  However, the U.S. dollar isn't likely to be bid sharply higher following a recent statement by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  that it will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to smooth out volatility arising from merger and acquisition-related outflows. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they expect the U.S. dollar to trade in a S$1.7530-$1.7580 range overnight. \par \par \uc2  On the bond market, prices of Singapore Government Securities ended lower, but off their intraday lows, on nervous selling triggered by the price of the 10-year bond falling below the key psychological level 108.00. \par \par \uc2  The consequent rise in bond yields pushed interest rate swaps higher, which, in turn, generated additional selling pressure on bonds, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Higher money market rates also encouraged bond selling. \par \par \uc2  Light buying toward the close of the session helped bonds close off their intraday lows. \par \par \uc2  The 10-year bond yield ended four basis points higher at 3.60% and the seven-year yield rose two basis points to 3.43%. \par \par \uc2  The yield on the five-year government paper was unchanged at 3.01% while the two-year bond yield was up one basis point at 2.11%. \par \par \uc2  In the interest rate swaps market, the most liquid seven-year yield was at 3.51%, compared with 3.48% Monday. The spread between the two-and 10-year swaps was at 125 basis points, resulting in a steep swap curve. \par \par \uc2  On the interbank money market, the overnight and term rates rose on the back of a weaker Singapore dollar, dealers said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                    Late Wednesday    Late Monday\par                  bid     offer     bid     offer\par Overnight       2.5000%  2.7500%   1.6250%  1.7500%\par 3 Month         1.8750%  2.0000%   1.7500%  1.8750%\par 1 Year          2.1250%  2.2500%   2.0000%  2.1250%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; (+65) 421-4809;\par amit.prakash@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx37010yb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
