\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore MAS To Control M&A-Related  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Volatility:Report \b0\par\par\uc2 411 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2001\par 05:38\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  -(Dow Jones)- The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to smooth out volatility arising from a large demand for U.S. dollars when a local company makes a sizable foreign acquisition, the Business Times reported Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's comments come a day after the U.S. dollar hit a three-month high against the Singapore dollar. The local unit fell on concerns that a successful bid for Australia's Cable & Wireless Optus Ltd. (A.CWO) by Singapore Telecommunications Ltd. (P.GST) would create a large demand for the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar hit a high of S$1.7555 in intraday trading Monday before closing Asian trading at S$1.7533. \par \par \uc2  "MAS recognizes mergers and acquisitions by local companies may cause heightened volatility in the S$ (Singapore dollar), even though they have no impact on the fundamentals of the Singapore economy," the newspaper quoted the MAS as saying. \par \par \uc2  "MAS will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if necessary to dampen excessive S$ exchange rate movements, including movements arising from funding of corporate M&A activities." \par \par \uc2  Singapore Telecom which is 80% government-owned, has emerged as a favorite to acquire Australia's second-largest phone company. Other bidders include Telecom Corp. of New Zealand (NZT) and Vodafone Pacific, part of Vodafone Group PLC (VOD). \par \par \uc2  Singapore Telecom could make a formal bid for Optus as early as this week, a person close to the transaction said Monday. \par \par \uc2  Officials from Singapore Telecom recently met the head of Australia's competition agency and didn't confront any potential problems, a person close to the transaction in Australia said. \par \par \uc2  SingTel's board is expected to put the finishing touches on a bid at its next board meeting. The company isn't believed to be too keen on offering a premium of more than 15% for Optus, valuing a bid at about A$16.7 billion. \par \par \uc2  The MAS said deals like the possible Optus purchase will involve selling some Singapore dollars, but such transactions won't lead to any changes in monetary policy. These transactions won't lead to changes in the undisclosed band within which the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's value is determined either. \par \par \uc2  The MAS uses the value of the Singapore dollar - determined against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  - when implementing monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  -By Hasan Jafri, Dow Jones Newswires; (+65) 9877-2407; hasan.jafri@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx3600zfj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
