\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Lower In Late Trade; MAS Fears Cap USD Rise \b0\par\par\uc2 525 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Februar 2001\par 09:22\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar was lower against the U.S. dollar late Monday, tracking a weaker yen, but its downside was capped by fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0808 GMT, the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.7474, up from S$1.7448 late Friday in Asia. The U.S. dollar traded in a narrow S$1.7468-80 range with trade light due to a public holiday in Japan. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar opened higher following its gains in London trading Friday after the Bank of Japan surprised markets by trimming its overnight deposit rate by 15 basis points to 0.35%. \par \par \uc2  The move was symbolic, and is expected to do little to stimulate the country's flagging economy. The yen fell to Y117.74 to the U.S. dollar after the announcement, from its opening level of Y116.70 Friday. \par \par \uc2  On Monday, the yen fell further to a low of Y117.92, dragging the Singapore dollar and most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower. \par \par \uc2  However, the Singapore dollar's downside was contained by fears that the MAS may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to defend the local unit if it fell S$1.7490-S$1.7500 to the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2  "I think \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is unlikely around S$1.75 given a weaker yen, corporates are still selling (U.S. dollars) around S$1.7490," a chief dealer at a European bank said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar is expected to trade in a S$1.7450-S$1.7500 range overnight, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  On the bond market, prices of Singapore government securities soared to new highs as interest rates eased further. Expectations of strong demand at a seven-year bond auction due later this month also fueled buying in the tenor, a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  The 10-year Singapore government bond yield fell seven basis points to 3.48% and the seven-year yield ended six basis points lower at 3.24%. \par \par \uc2  The yield on the five-year issue fell seven basis points to 2.93%, while the two-year bond yield fell eight basis points to 2.03%. \par \par \uc2  All yields ended at record lows. \par \par \uc2  The above yields are pre-settlement closing bids and may be revised slightly on settlement. \par \par \uc2  In the swap market, interest rate swaps eased following lower bond yields. \par \par \uc2  The 10-year interest rate swaps fell four basis points to 3.66% while the seven-year fell six basis points to 3.38%. The spreads between the two- and 10-year interest rate swaps widened, resulting in a steeper swap curve. \par \par \uc2  On the interbank money market, the overnight offered rate eased after the weekend while terms rates eased amid flush liquidity and prospects for a marked slowdown in the local and global economies. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                  Late Monday       Late Friday\par                  bid     offer     bid     offer\par Overnight       2.3750%  2.5000%   2.3750%  2.5000%\par 3 Month         1.8750%  2.0000%   1.8750%  2.0000%\par 1 Year          2.1250%  2.0000%   2.1250%  2.2500%\par -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; (+65) 421-4809;\par amit.prakashdowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx2c00m4u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
