\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Up Late On MAS Fears,Yen Rise; Bonds End Up \b0\par\par\uc2 377 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Februar 2001\par 11:40\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar was higher late Thursday, recovering from overnight losses amid fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and tracking the yen's recovery from its intraday low, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 1006 GMT (5:06 a.m. EST), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.7466, down from S$1.7473 late Wednesday in Asia. \par \par \uc2  Against the yen, it was quoted at Y116.28, down from Thursday's intraday high of Y116.57, and lower than Y116.37 late Wednesday in New York. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar found it difficult to extend its rally beyond its overnight high of S$1.7485 and retreated in early trading Thursday on profit-taking to around S$1.7475-80, amid fears that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if it approaches S$1.7500. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's retreat from its intraday high against the yen prompted further selling of U.S. dollars for the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the U.S. dollar will likely stay bid above S$1.7455-60 as local banks and companies are expected to buy on dips. Its upside is expected to be capped around S$1.7480-S$1.7500 on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears. \par \par \uc2  On the bond market, prices of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  Government Securities rose slightly on bargain hunting after recent profit-taking following record high prices set Monday. \par \par \uc2  Market participants were keen buyers on dips amid expectations that local and global interest rates will stay soft in coming months. \par \par \uc2  The 10-year Singapore government bond yield fell one basis point to 3.61% and the seven-year yield ended two basis points lower at 3.34%. \par \par \uc2  The yield on the five-year issue fell three basis points to 3.03%, while the two-year bond yield stayed flat at 2.16%. \par \par \uc2  On the interbank money market, interest rates eased slightly amid flush liquidity. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                  Late Thursday      Late Wednesday\par                  bid     offer     bid     offer\par Overnight       1.5000%  1.7500%   1.7500%  1.8750%\par 3 Month         1.9375%  2.0625%   1.9375%  2.2500%\par 1 Year          2.1250%  2.2500%   2.2500%  2.3750%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; (+65) 421-4809;\par amit.prakash@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010725dx2800kn8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
