\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  - A touch firmer before FOMC meets. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,603 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Januar 2001\par 09:46\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Jan 30 (Reuters) - Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were slightly firmer late Tuesday as market players headed for the sidelines ahead of the Federal Reserve's two-day meeting, starting later in the day. \par \par \uc2  Trading in regional markets was generally quiet, as investors exercised caution ahead of the meeting. A recent Reuters poll of top Wall Street bond dealers showed a unanimous expectation of a 50 basis point cut, given Fed chairman Alan Greenspan's recent somber comments on the slowing U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  Analysts were generally of the opinion that another rate cut by the Fed would be good news for the region, as it will increase global liquidity and allow regional central banks to also ease monetary policy. \par \par \uc2  Some added that while U.S. growth is expected to slow sharply in the first half of the year, the Fed's expected easing should help spur growth during the second half. \par \par \uc2  "Prospect of a 50 basis point Fed rate cut (Wednesday) is strengthening the second half of 2001 U.S. recovery story," Standard Chartered Bank said in a daily research note. \par \par \uc2  This week will also see the release of a spate of U.S. economic data. U.S. consumer confidence data for January is scheduled to be issued on Tuesday, advance fourth-quarter gross domestic product data on Wednesday, and the January employment report on Friday. \par \par \uc2  The euro lost some of its lustre as investors bet the Fed would secure not only a soft landing for the U.S. economy, but a rapid re-acceleration as well. \par \par \uc2  Even the yen managed a four-week high against the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , pushing the dollar down to test major support at 116.15 yen , before it climbed back up in late Asian trade. \par \par \uc2  INDONESIAN RUPIAH \par \par \uc2  The rupiah spent most of the day on the wrong side of 9,400, though relative political calm staved off further weakness. \par \par \uc2  Dealers in Jakarta said local banks shed some long dollar positions early in the session as state bank sales of around $10 million late Monday caught many short on rupiah. \par \par \uc2  However, the rupiah found it difficult to break the 9,400 resistance given local corporate demand for dollars and fears the political situation in Jakarta could turn violent. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian parliament will decide on Thursday the course of action to take on two financial scandals linked to President Abdurrahman Wahid, which may ignite a brewing political battle between Wahid and his critics. \par \par \uc2  Large demonstrations on Monday sent shivers through Jakarta's financial markets as players fretted over possible violence between Wahid's supporters and his detractors. \par \par \uc2  The country's defence minister warned on Tuesday the military could seize power if political leaders did not end their bickering. President Wahid appeared on national television on Tuesday to plead his innocence and cool tensions. \par \par \uc2  KOREAN WON -2 The won was unable to extend its gains beyond an early high of 1,258.0 per dollar as importers bought dollars in a thin market, where most players were keen to unload long positions ahead of the Fed meeting. \par \par \uc2  There was little immediate reaction to news Korea's industrial output slowed for a fourth straight month, but analysts said mounting evidence of a flagging economy strengthened the case for a rate cut by the Bank of Korea. \par \par \uc2  Industrial production fell a seasonally adjusted 2.7 percent in December from a month earlier due to a contraction in consumption, facilities investment and weak domestic sales. \par \par \uc2  Earlier this month, the Bank of Korea left the overnight call rate target steady at 5.25 percent, in what was seen as a sign of its commitment to corporate reform. \par \par \uc2  But many analysts are now predicting a 25 basis point cut at its next meeting on February 8. \par \par \uc2  "We think that more active use of fiscal policy will be required by the government to try and soft land the economy," HSBC said in a research report. \par \par \uc2  "Consequently we remain bullish dollar/won to 1,400 before a rebound in H2 on dollar weakness, and also look for the BOK call rate to fall 100 basis points this year," it said. \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar struggled to maintain its early firmness, but succumbed to short covering of U.S. dollar positions in late trading. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the Sing dollar made slight gains at the opening mostly on the back of the firmer yen. \par \par \uc2  However, lingering weakness in the euro weighed on the local dollar. The Sing dollar is managed against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , of which the yen and euro are believed to be major components. \par \par \uc2  But, trading was generally cautious, with dealers unwilling to take large positions ahead of the Fed's rate decision. \par \par \uc2  The local dollar traded in a relatively tight range of 1.7430-1.7476. Dealers said the U.S. dollar's gains continued to be capped around 1.7500 due to concerns about possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 . They added there was selling interest around that area as well. \par \par \uc2  The MAS said last week it was reviewing its 2001 inflation and growth forecasts given the negative external environment and would release a monetary policy statement in February. \par \par \uc2  This sparked speculation it might shift to a neutral stance from a tightening bias, but dealers were still worried the authorities may frown upon any big downward movement in the local dollar. \par \par \uc2  Chartists said the daily chart showed dollar/Sing in an uptrend channel since December 22. \par \par \uc2  Looking ahead, dealers said they expected the Sing dollar to consolidate around this area, with the session's low of 1.7430 seen as resistance. \par \par \uc2  THAI BAHT \par \par \uc2  The baht was supported within a narrow range for most of the day as offshore players bought it against other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the Singapore dollar. \par \par \uc2  But the upside was capped on dollar demand from local corporates and importers which pulled the baht down to its day low of 42.75. \par \par \uc2  The baht funding cost in the offshore market also rose with one-month quoted at -4.0/-1.0 satang from -6.5/-4.0 earlier in the day. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said sentiment was improving on expectations the winner of the January 6 polls Thaksin Shinawatra would be able to give Thailand a stable government. \par \par \uc2  A senior economist at HSBC said he expected the baht to firm to around 40 per dollar by the end of 2001 from 42.7 this week and around 44.0 a few months ago. \par \par \uc2  Global banking giant HSBC saw the Thai current account surplus narrowing to 5.2 percent of gross domestic product in 2001 from 6.7 percent in 2000 and 9.1 percent a year earlier. \par \par \uc2  Thaksin said a baht exchange rate of slightly over 42 per dollar was acceptable and would not be too strong for exports. \par \par \uc2  PHILIPPINE PESO \par \par \uc2  The peso fell to its day low of 49.65 before closing at 49.60 as demand for the dollar rose from importers and corporates seeking to cover their month-end inventory requirements. \par \par \uc2  Earlier in the day the peso touched a high of 49.15, compared to Monday's onshore close of 49.48. Turnover was down to $110.55 million, from $126 million on Monday. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the absence of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which had been buying dollars to meet foreign debt commitments, and some residual of remittances from overseas workers had helped the peso rise in early trade. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has repeatedly denied making dollar purchases to repay loans and bond maturities estimated at around $400 million falling in January and February. \par \par \uc2  The peso was lifted from around 50 to a dollar on Monday as overseas worker remittances flowed into the market after the Chinese New Year holidays in the region last week. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the peso was likely to trade in a broad 49.00-49.75 range in the coming days. \par \par \uc2  TAIWAN DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar firmed up, helped by a rising yen and gains in local stocks, but its upside was capped by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers in \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was spotted buying dollars at around the 32.40 level to keep the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from appreciating too much, especially against the yen and the won. Japan and Korea compete with Taiwan in several export markets. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was believed to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Monday too, to prevent the local dollar strengthening past 32.50, but its absence early on Tuesday emboldened the market. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said strong foreign portfolio inflows and hopes of a rally after a widely expected cut in U.S. rates were likely to support the Taiwan dollar. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  0844 GMT \par \par \uc2   Change on the day \pa