\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Dn Late, MAS Action Suspected, Cos Buy USD \b0\par\par\uc2 489 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 9 Januar 2001\par 10:18\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2001 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar was weaker against its U.S. counterpart late Tuesday on suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and corporate demand for U.S. dollars, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0901 GMT (4:01 a.m. EST), the U.S. dollar was trading at its intraday high of S$1.7326, up from S$1.7298 late Monday in Asia. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar opened lower, hitting an intraday low of S$1.7277, in line with its weakness against most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly the yen. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar's early decline attracted local companies, mostly importers, to start buying the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  midmorning, and the buying intensified in the afternoon, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Most of the buying was through local banks and there is talk that some of it could have been on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said a dealer at a U.S. brokerage. "This would have been a good opportunity for them (\b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , if indeed they did," the dealer added. \par \par \uc2  The MAS rarely comments on its \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. In recent weeks, the MAS has been suspected of buying U.S. dollars to prevent the excessive strengthening of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The MAS said late Monday that Singapore's official foreign reserves at the end of December stood at US$80.36 billion, up 2.9% from November. Market participants say the increase in reserves likely reflects recent U.S. dollar buying by the MAS. \par \par \uc2  Dealers expect the U.S. dollar to remain capped at S$1.7330-40 overnight, with initial support around S$1.7300 and then at S$1.7270. \par \par \uc2  On the bond market, prices of Singapore government securities fell on profit-taking following the steep overnight fall in U.S. Treasurys, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The yield on the 10-year Singapore government bond ended five basis points higher at 3.82%, the seven-year bond yield rose five basis points to 3.61%, the five-year yield rose one basis point to 3.35% and the two-year bond yield rose by one basis point to 2.78%. \par \par \uc2  In the money market, the overnight offered rate was slightly higher but most other interbank rates edged lower amid greater lending interest. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the impact of last week's 50 basis points rate cut by the U.S. Federal Reserve has trickled through to the Singapore money market, but rates aren't expected to fall sharply as they are close to historic lows. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                   Late Tuesday       Late Monday\par                   bid      offer     bid      offer\par Overnight         1.7500%  1.8750%   1.1250%  1.3750%\par 3 Month           2.5000%  2.5625%   2.5625%  2.6875%\par 1 Year            2.5625%  2.6875%   2.6250%  2.7500%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; (+65) 421-4809;\par amit.prakash@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010711dx19004bg\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
