\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  - North Asia dismayed, baht bounces. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,617 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Dezember 2000\par 08:27\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Dec 22 (Reuters) - North Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  had few good tidings on the Friday before Christmas as sentiment soured over domestic woes. \par \par \uc2  Most markets in Asia will be closed for Christmas on Monday, with Taiwan also shut for Constitution Day. This report will resume on Tuesday, December 26. \par \par \uc2  Concerns over slowing growth in the U.S. and Japan loomed over the region, but weighed especially hard on North Asian markets. Both Korea and Taiwan are viewed as being particularly exposed to the technology sector's slowdown. \par \par \uc2  In Seoul, some 16,000 unionised bank workers took to the streets to protest government-led restructuring efforts. \par \par \uc2  Korean markets have been fretting over the possibility that ongoing labour unrest may delay progress in much needed reforms. \par \par \uc2  Authorities in Korea and Taiwan moved in to support their sagging \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  with verbal and actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Dealers also noted that thin markets across the region distorted otherwise small bids and offers. \par \par \uc2  Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  fared a little better, with the Thai baht's unexpected year-end star turn extending for another session. But analysts were divided as to whether it would last next year, with some pointing out that pre-holiday markets were not a reliable barometer of sentiment. \par \par \uc2  Others said this could be a turning point for the baht, and other Southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that have been troubled by a year of political turmoil and shaky signs of economic recovery. \par \par \uc2  The euro was the only real winner, defying long-term bears with its climb against the yen and dollar. \par \par \uc2  KOREAN WON -2 \par \par \uc2  The won was rescued from a day low of 1,237.5 after state banks entered the market to sell around $150 million to support the ailing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  A dealer in \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  said there was also talk of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  directly in the market at 1,233. \par \par \uc2  The won - which has lost around eight percent of its value since the start of this year - was weighed by problems both at home and abroad. \par \par \uc2  Protracted declines in the Nasdaq raised concerns over the global tech sector. At home, Kookmin Bank and Houseing and Commercial Bank unions went on strike despite the government's promise not to force the two to merge. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the strike underlined difficulties faced by the government in pushing ahead with much-needed reforms. \par \par \uc2  TAIWAN DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar sagged anew as the stock market reversed early gains to hit a fresh 57-month low on heavy selling triggered by rising margin calls after the TAIEX's seven-session losing streak. \par \par \uc2  Buying by state funds and microchip foundry United Microelectronics' announcement that it would buy back 400 million shares between December 27, 2000 and February 26, 2001 helped the TAIEX finish just 0.12 percent lower. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  also cushioned the Taiwan dollar's fall, but dealers expected further declines as Thursday's weaker than expected industrial output for November had only confirmed the picture of a slowing economy. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's toughened stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading kept players cautious amid rumours it had temporarily barred several foreign and local banks from trading in forwards. \par \par \uc2  A \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official declined to comment on the rumours, but said the bank would punish commercial banks found to have broken the rules, noting that this could include a suspension. \par \par \uc2  Active dollar buying in the non-deliverable forward market, which has pushed six-month NDF rates to more than T$1 over the spot price, has pressured Taiwan's exchange rate despite frequent support from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  THAI BAHT \par \par \uc2  The baht marched higher, flirting with resistance at 42 to the dollar on the back of dollar offers from funds and interbank players, with gains exaggerated by a thin pre-holiday market. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  repeatedly failed to break the 42 barrier, which represents a 62 percent Fibonacci retracement of the dollar's August 23-November 21 upmove. \par \par \uc2  "A few funds are selling dollars through local banks, prompting some interbank players to trim long dollar positions. And the market is so thin, hence the magnitude of its rise," said a dealer at a U.S. bank in Bangkok. "Players are also cautious to build fresh long dollar positions at this point given it's the year-end and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is monitoring trade closely ahead of the election," he said. \par \par \uc2  There was a vague rumour the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was in the market to sell dollars, but a number of dealers dismissed it as unlikely. The bank had itself denied such talk on Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  "If the government needs to do anything at all in the market, they'd probably buy rather than sell the dollar because they need to repay IMF loans," said another Bangkok dealer. \par \par \uc2  Charts show that if the 42.00 level is breached, the baht will face another strong resistance at 41.50 which is the 38 percent retracement of the dollar's broad rise between January 3 and November 21 this year. \par \par \uc2  Political developments are likely to take the limelight next week, with the National Counter Corruption Commission expected to rule on cases against Prime Minister Chuan Leekpai, political front runner Thaksin Shinawatra, and Deputy Prime Minister Banyat Bantadtan of false declaration of assets. Analysts believed Chuan is likely to be cleared, while cases against Thaksin, who is tipped to be the next prime minister, and Banyat, are more serious. If they are found guilty, they will be banned from politics for five years. \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The Sing dollar found a toehold above 1.7300 in a thin afternoon market, with one British name seen buying the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  actively. \par \par \uc2  It had briefly dipped to the day's low of 1.7350 on dollar bids from a local bank before offers from other banks and a few local companies reversed its course. The Sing dollar was also buoyed by the perky euro, believed to be a substantial component in its trade-weighted basket. \par \par \uc2  Gains were well-capped at 1.7260, indicating players were cautious the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might step in to curb a sharp rise. \par \par \uc2  The government is expected to release November consumer price data at 1030 GMT on Saturday. \par \par \uc2  Analysts expect mild inflation ahead for Singapore. A Reuters poll of 10 economists forecast inflation at 1.41 percent on average for 2000 and 2.01 percent in 2001. \par \par \uc2  INDONESIAN RUPIAH \par \par \uc2  The rupiah dragged its feet the wrong side of 9,350 for most of the day, sandwiched between dollar bids from corporate and offshore players and offers from state banks in a thin pre-holiday market. \par \par \uc2  Traditional year-end inertia was worsened by caution ahead of a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meeting with local and foreign bank dealers. \par \par \uc2  A Bank Indonesia official said deputy governor Miranda Goeltom would meet with dealers around 0630 GMT. \par \par \uc2  The topic of discussion was unclear, though traders in Jakarta said it was likely to focus on a credit facility for \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  loans to banks and the corporate sector. \par \par \uc2  Separately, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Sjahril Sabirin said the bank did not want to raise interest rates excessively, adding that it was difficult to predict the rate trend early next year because of a variety of uncertainties. On Wednesday, the bank's benchmark SBI certificates were hiked to 14.53 percent, continuing a gradual uptrend of recent months that has sparked some worries about the impact on the embattled corporate sector. \par \par \uc2  The market was unimpressed by Finance Minister Prijadi Praptosuhardjo's forecast on Thursday, that gross domestic product growth could reach 5.5-6.0 percent next year, exceeding the state budget target of five percent. \par \par \uc2  A research note from SEB Merchant Banking described the finance minister as "ambitious and optimistic" in his assessment. \par \par \uc2  PHILIPPINE PESO \par \par \uc2  Trade in the peso was moribund with domestic players keen to square up before the Christmas weekend. \par \par \uc2  The peso managed to poke above the 50 per dollar mark, but remained well within Thursday's already narrow band, on meagre end-year volume of $57.9 million. \par \par \uc2  Chartists said narrowing price action in dollar/peso had created a constricting triangle on the daily chart and a break to the upside was likely soon. \par \par \uc2  Analysts expect the peso to weaken again in early 2001 as the prop of overseas worker remittances subsides, and the market re-focuses on political and economic turmoil within the country. \par \par \uc2  Even data showing the sixth straight month of growth in production indices for October failed to excite peso traders. \par \par \uc2  The value of production index surged 31.7 percent year-on-year while the volume of production index leapt 21 percent on higher output from oil refiners, food manufacturing and electrical machinery. \par \par \uc2  Economists attributed the rise to seasonal pre-Christmas rises in production and said output was likely taper off in November and December. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  0724 GMT \par \par \uc2   Change on the day \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2     Latest bid     Prev Close    Pct Move \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Japan yen     112.53\tab  112.68\tab    +0.13\par  Sing dlr\tab 1.7260\tab  1.7305\tab    +0.26\par  Taiwan dlr    33.183\tab  33.172\tab    -0.03\par  Korean won   1231.00\tab 1225.00\tab    -0.49\par  Baht\tab      42.03\tab   42.39\tab    +0.87\par  Peso\tab      49.94\tab   50.00\tab    +0.12\par  Rupiah\tab  9335.00\tab 9290.00\tab    -0.48\par  Ringgit\tab  3.7995\tab  3.7995\tab    +0.00\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Change on year\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \tab Latest     End prev yr     Pct Move\par  Japan yen     112.53\tab  102.21\tab    -9.17\par  Sing dlr\tab 1.7260\tab  1.6640\tab    -3.59\par  Taiwan dlr    33.183\tab  31.380\tab    -5.43\par  Korean won   1231.00\tab 1135.00\tab    -7.80\par  Baht\tab      42.03\tab   37.45\tab   -10.89\par  Peso\tab      49.94\tab   40.20\tab   -19.50\par  Rupiah\tab  9335.00\tab 6975.00\tab   -25.28\par  Ringgit\tab  3.7995\tab  3.7997\tab    +0.01.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dwcm09ffy\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
