\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  - N.Asia gloomy, baht, Sing dlr buoyed. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,361 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Dezember 2000\par 10:29\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Dec 21 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  went separate ways in late trade on Thursday, driven by year-end needs and local factors. \par \par \uc2  The Korean won and Taiwan dollar, whose performance has been linked closely to their stock performance, led regional losers as another savaging on Wall Street sent local bourses reeling. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht stole the limelight, gaining more than one percent at one point on the back of big dollar offers from a major global fund believed to be covering short baht positions. \par \par \uc2  Asian markets had little reaction to U.S. president-designate George W. Bush's choice of Treasury Secretary. Bush on Wednesday gave the important portfolio to Paul O'Neill, head of the world's largest aluminium company. \par \par \uc2  Although O'Neill won a glowing endorsement from Fed Chairman Alan Greenspan, analysts are still digesting whether his manufacturing background would lead him to favour a lower dollar as a boost to U.S. exports. \par \par \uc2  The euro jumped to four-month highs against the dollar for the second consecutive day on Thursday, cashing in on growing concerns about a rapid slowdown in the U.S. economy. The yen climbed off its 16-month lows of around 113.15 on dollar profit taking. Chartists said if the dollar could hold above 113, the next target could easily be 115. \par \par \uc2  THAI BAHT \par \par \uc2  The baht made strong gains as one global fund was believed to be buying the unit aggressively in the past few days in relatively thin trade to cover short positions. \par \par \uc2  Dealers in Bangkok said the fund sold around $200-300 million through one European bank alone. \par \par \uc2  A break above 42.50 - which had not been pierced since October 10 - also prompted exporters and other players to sell the dollar to guard against further losses. \par \par \uc2  But the baht pared earlier gains in the afteroon on news that Thai banks made little or no progress restructuring their billions of dollars in bad loans in November, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Three of the country's top banks actually reported small increases in their levels of non-performing loans (NPLs) - loans yielding no income for at least three months. \par \par \uc2  Baht funding costs nudged up, with one-month forward rates quoted at -2/1 satang per dollar from -3/-1 satang earlier. The overnight interbank rate was soft at 1.75-2.50 percent. \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar followed the baht higher, though trading was extremely thin leaving it vulnerable to any shifts. \par \par \uc2  "It's just the year-end situation with the market not really willing to hold on to any (U.S.) dollars. But they're caught short now and then," said a dealer at a European bank. \par \par \uc2  With few players in the market, a local bank's U.S. dollar selling in early trading brought the Sing dollar to a high of 1.7255. But dealers said some U.S. dollar buying interest emerged around these levels. \par \par \uc2  Dealers also pointed out that with the euro as well as regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  firmer, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  was less likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the Sing has not appreciated too much against its trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  INDONESIAN RUPIAH \par \par \uc2  The rupiah saw its gains from earlier in the week slip away as onshore and offshore players bought back dollars. \par \par \uc2  Dealers in Jakarta said several onshore interbank players were caught short, having held off on buying dollars as state banks and the Indonesian Bank Restructuring Agency sold off the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier in the week. \par \par \uc2  However, neither the state banks nor IBRA came into the market to sell dollars, spurring others to take advantage of the relatively cheap levels. \par \par \uc2  Dealers added that foreign banks onshore bought around $30-$50 million, as dollar bids gathered momentum after it breached 9,275. \par \par \uc2  The market shrugged off Indonesian President Abdurrahman Wahid's denial of an impending cabinet reshuffle amid media reports that several ministers were expected to quit over disappointment with his leadership. Wahid's over one-year old administration saw a cabinet reshuffle in August. \par \par \uc2  PHILIPPINE PESO \par \par \uc2  The peso withstood the government's downward revisions to growth forecasts for next year and the latest twists in President Joseph Estrada's impeachment trial, ending at 50.045, little changed from Wednesday's 50.058 close. \par \par \uc2  Volume was light at $88.5 million and trade confined to a tight 50.045-50.105 range. Chartists said the narrowing price action in the dollar/peso had created a constricting triangle on the daily chart, with a break to the upside likely soon. \par \par \uc2  Analysts too have said the peso is likely to head south again in the new year, once the prop of seasonal remittances from overseas workers disappears and the country's political and economic woes return to centrestage. \par \par \uc2  Socio-economic Planning Secretary Felipe Medalla said gross domestic product was expected to be between 3.0-3.5 percent next year if the impeachment process was concluded in the first quarter. The government had previously predicted 3.5 percent growth next year, down from an earlier 4.0-4.5 percent forecast. \par \par \uc2  TAIWAN DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar sank to a 22-month low against the U.S. dollar on Thursday despite \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , depressed by hefty Nasdaq-inspired losses in local stocks. \par \par \uc2  Dealers in Taipei said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was believed to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  at $T33.175 which the dollar repeatedly failed to breach. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was likely to be aimed at curbing volatility and not at boosting the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  because Taiwan would need a weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support its slowing economy. \par \par \uc2  Data from the island's economics ministry on Thursday showed that export orders had slowed in November from October despite reaching a traditional peak season for mainstay electronics products. \par \par \uc2  However, year-on-year growth of 14.72 percent still exceeded analysts' expectations. A Reuters poll of 10 economists yielded an average forecast of a 13.09 percent rise. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan's industrial output also rose a slower than expected 1.63 percent on year in November due to sluggish domestic consumption. The government forecast five percent output growth in 2001, below its 2000 forecast of over eight percent growth. \par \par \uc2  KOREAN WON -2 \par \par \uc2  The won closed at a 21-month low, spooked by the stock market's recent declines and a planned bank strike on Friday. \par \par \uc2  Unions of six banks involved in merger plans were expected to start a general strike on Friday, which union officials said could cripple \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  of 30 percent of the banking sector. \par \par \uc2  The government declared the strike illegal and vowed stern action if it went ahead, but union leaders showed little sign of backing down, setting the stage for what could be the biggest confrontation between labour and government since the crisis. \par \par \uc2  It said nearly 6,000 riot police would be mobilised to counter the planned strike. \par \par \uc2  Data released on Thursday showed the seasonally adjusted jobless rate rose to 4.1 percent in November, up from 3.9 percent in October but down from 4.9 percent the year before. \par \par \uc2  The won's drop to its lowest level since March 17, 1999 spurred a verbal warning from a \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  official, but dealers said there was no sign of actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  0928 GMT \par \par \uc2   Change on the day \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2     Latest bid     Prev Close    Pct Move \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Japan yen     112.38\tab  112.84\tab    +0.41\par  Sing dlr\tab 1.7296\tab  1.7344\tab    +0.28\par  Taiwan dlr    33.172\tab  33.129\tab    -0.13\par  Korean won   1227.80\tab 1217.00\tab    -0.88\par  Baht\tab      42.35\tab   42.74\tab    +0.92\par  Peso\tab      50.00\tab   50.00\tab    +0.00\par  Rupiah\tab  9250.00\tab 9220.00\tab    -0.32\par  Ringgit\tab  3.7995\tab  3.7995\tab    +0.00\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Change on year\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \tab Latest     End prev yr     Pct Move\par  Japan yen     112.38\tab  102.21\tab    -9.05\par  Sing dlr\tab 1.7296\tab  1.6640\tab    -3.79\par  Taiwan dlr    33.172\tab  31.380\tab    -5.40\par  Korean won   1227.80\tab 1135.00\tab    -7.56\par  Baht\tab      42.35\tab   37.45\tab   -11.57\par  Peso\tab      50.00\tab   40.20\tab   -19.60\par  Rupiah\tab  9250.00\tab 6975.00\tab   -24.59\par  Ringgit\tab  3.7995\tab  3.7997\tab    +0.01.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dwcl09b5m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
