\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Higher Late, Tracks Baht, Rupiah; Bonds Dn \b0\par\par\uc2 393 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Dezember 2000\par 09:57\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar was slightly higher against its U.S. counterpart late Wednesday, fluctuating in a narrow range in tandem with the Indonesian rupiah and the Thai baht. \par \par \uc2  Around 0840 GMT (3:40 a.m. EST), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.7330, down from S$1.7335 late Tuesday in Asia. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar opened slightly higher in line with its overnight gains, but its losses against the baht and the rupiah pushed it lower against the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as well. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves were largely flow driven and somewhat exaggerated in a thin year-end market. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said that the U.S. dollar's fall below support at S$1.7330 was short-lived given fears that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy U.S. dollars. The MAS is believed to be uncomfortable with the U.S. dollar's rapid decline against the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent weeks. \par \par \uc2  Dealers expect the U.S. dollar to mostly trade in a S$1.7330-S$1.7380 range in the near term. However, if the U.S. dollar does stay below S$1.7330, dealers expect it will be strongly supported at S$1.7300. \par \par \uc2  In the bond market, prices of longer-dated Singapore Government Securities ended slightly lower amid thin trading. \par \par \uc2  The longer bonds were bid lower, but few trades took place because of a lack of selling interest, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Bond prices have risen in recent months and banks are comfortable holding bonds at current levels, at least until the end of the year. \par \par \uc2  The yields on the five-, seven- and 10-year bonds ended one to two basis points higher each at 3.51%, 3.84% and 4.13%, respectively. \par \par \uc2  In the money market, the overnight offered rate edged higher to 2.625% from 2.5625% late Tuesday, but was within its normal trading range. However, term rates remained steady amid ample liquidity in the banking system. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                   Late Wednesday       Late Tuesday\par                   bid      offer     bid      offer\par Overnight         2.5000%  2.6250%   2.4375%  2.5625%\par 3 Month           2.7500%  2.8750%   2.7500%  2.8750%\par 1 Year            2.8125%  2.9375%   2.8125%  2.9375%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; (65) 421-4809;\par amit.prakash@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dwck060lr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
