\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  - Steady early on, but concerns linger. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,871 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Dezember 2000\par 02:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Dec 15 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were mostly steady early on Friday, taking some respite from overnight declines in the U.S. dollar against the euro and the yen. \par \par \uc2  Subdued year-end trading, weekend squaring of long dollar positions and fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  could also provide support to some of the regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  But growing evidence of a slowing U.S. economy and Thursday's fall on Wall Street amid worries about corporate earnings were likely to cap any gains. \par \par \uc2  Sentiment in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is already weighed by domestic political worries, slow pace of corporate and financial restructuring and on an expected slowing of growth and exports amid peaking global demand. \par \par \uc2  Profit warnings from financial giants Chase Manhattan Corp and J.P. Morgan pulled the Dow Jones industrial average down 1.11 percent while a cut in revenue forecast by software leader Microsoft wiped 3.34 percent off the technology-laden Nasdaq Composite Index . \par \par \uc2  With the victory for Republican George W. Bush in the presidential race already discounted, markets returned their focus to the gloomy corporate earnings outlook and impact from a slowing economy. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. Labor Department said on Thursday its Producer Price Index, a key measure of inflation at the wholesale level, inched up only 0.1 percent, following a 0.4 percent increase in October and matching market expectations. \par \par \uc2  The core PPI, that excludes more volatile food and energy prices, was unchanged from the prior month after a decline of 0.1 percent in October. \par \par \uc2  Markets would be watchful of U.S. November consumer prices due Friday. \par \par \uc2  But analysts said the flat core rate suggested receding threat of inflation and could prompt the Federal Reserve to shift its policy stance to neutral in its December 19 meeting. \par \par \uc2  The euro poked higher in early Tokyo trade, closing in on the $0.8974 major resistance mark against the dollar and was at around four-month highs against the yen. \par \par \uc2  The yen gained from dollar profit-taking in late New York trade, coming off its new 16-month lows against the dollar but was still perched around the 112 mark. \par \par \uc2  However, analysts said the overall dollar strength remained intact while the yen was likely to remain broadly weak which should keep most of the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  under pressure. \par \par \uc2  KOREAN WON -2 \par \par \uc2  The won was likely to come under renewed pressure on the back of another fall in technology stocks in the U.S. \par \par \uc2  Adding more woe to the U.S. technology sector, U.S. software giant Microsoft Corp. said on Thursday after the market's close that it would miss its quarterly earnings and revenue targets due to the global slowdwon, lower personal computer sales and less corporate spending. \par \par \uc2  Investors on the tech-dominated Korea stock market were likely to continue pulling out of local tech stocks as Korea braced for a more dramatic slowdown than expected. \par \par \uc2  Another setback in the country's corporate and financial restructuring also hit local sentiment as Kookmin Bank announced on Thursday it had suspended merger talks with Housing and Commercial Bank . \par \par \uc2  Concerns over the reforms have been dragging local investor sentimemt lower in recent weeks. \par \par \uc2  On the non-deliverable forward market, the one-month won was at 1,201/2 and the three-month was at 1,201/2. \par \par \uc2  INDONESIAN RUPIAH \par \par \uc2  The rupiah was supported by fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and expectations of higher interest rate triggered by a recent spike in inflation. \par \par \uc2  Bank Indonesia has repeatedly warned the market recently that it was ready to supply dollars to ease downward pressure on the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also said on Wednesday the inflation rate for this year was expected to be 9-10 percent, the first official indication the figure could reach double digits, prompting some to speculate the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might have to hike rates soon. \par \par \uc2  But the rupiah's gains could be capped by another rumour that the IMF would delay the next disbursement of loans to Indonesia because the government missed a number of key reform commitments, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "The talk in the market now is that the funds will not be blocked but it will be delayed. It won't be disbursed in December," said a dealer at a U.S. bank in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  However, a senior IMF official told Reuters in Jakarta earlier this week that its $5.0 billion assistance programme to Indonesia was not under threat and the next letter of intent paving the way for future loans was being drawn up. \par \par \uc2  TAIWAN DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  Market players were set to test the Taiwan dollar's support of 33.10, but were likely to meet with stiff resistance from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  A dealer at a U.S. bank in Singapore had said the bank was believed to have made up 50 to 70 percent of the daily total volumes in the onshore \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market this week. \par \par \uc2  Dealers in Taipei have, however, noted that foreign institutional investors have bought a net total of T$13.614 billion of local stocks over the past eight sessions, lending some support to the local dollar. \par \par \uc2  But sentiment towards the local dollar remains poor as the key technology sector was expected to see slowing growth in the new year. Microsoft's profit warning was likely to send new jitters through Taiwan's retail investor-driven stock market. \par \par \uc2  Dealers in both Hong Kong and Singapore have said the market believed that only the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was propping up the local dollar in the face of slowing growth, ongoing problems in the banking sector, and doubts over government policies. \par \par \uc2  They also have said higher rates across the board in the NDF market accurately reflected the sentiment, as the spot rate has not been allowed to move according to market forces. \par \par \uc2  Short-term NDF rates have also risen recently as U.S. dollar buying continues in the market. The one-month Taiwan dollar NDF was at 0.32/37 and the three-month was at 0.510/610. \par \par \uc2  THAI BAHT \par \par \uc2  The baht was driven up by tight liquidity in the market which forced players to unwind long dollar/baht positions. \par \par \uc2  Although some participants sold forward contracts on rally to cash in on recent gains, baht demand and hence its funding cost, remain high, particularly in the offshore market. \par \par \uc2  The one-month forward rate in the offshore market rose to 10.7/20.7 satang per dollar in early trade compared with -7.0/-3.0 satang last Friday. \par \par \uc2  The onshore rate of the same tenor edged up to -4/-2 satang from -4.5/-4.0 satang late on Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The bankruptcy court is expected to reconvene on Friday to decide on a landmark $3.8 billion debt restructuring plan for Thai Petrochemical Industry Plc . \par \par \uc2  The delay was caused by a petition by TPI founder and chief executive Prachai Leopairatana, who is bitterly opposed to the debt plan, to replace the judges presiding over the case. \par \par \uc2  The restructuring deal involves converting nearly $800 million in unpaid interest to a 75 percent stake for creditors which include Thailand's largest commercial bank Bangkok Bank . \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar was supported on profit-taking in U.S. dollars led by some foreign banks, but dealers said fears of U.S. dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MAS) at around 1.7350 were likely to cap gains. \par \par \uc2  Dealers have cited local banks buying U.S. dollars on behalf of the MAS to temper recent gains in the Sing that pushed it to a five-week high of 1.7300 last Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the MAS had not changed its policy of "gradual appreciation" of the Sing against the undisclosed trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that it uses to manage the exchange rate. \par \par \uc2  But it appeared the authority might be concerned about the Sing dollar appreciating excessively against the basket, given the recent declines in the yen, won and the Taiwan dollar. \par \par \uc2  Japan, Korea and Taiwan are competitors of Singapore's major exports. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said overnight gains in the euro, which also had a significant weighting in the basket, could prompt the MAS to ease off its pressure on the Sing dollar. \par \par \uc2  Another factor that could help the Sing dollar was an early announcement by Taiwan's United Microelectronics Corp which said it had signed a memorandum of understanding with Infineon Technologies AG to jointly invest US$3.6 billion to build a 12-inch wafer plant in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  Infineon is a microchip unit of Germany's Siemens . \par \par \uc2  On the macro front, October retail sales data is expected at 1030 GMT. The September retail sales index rose 16.9 percent on the year, and was up 5.5 percent over August 2000. \par \par \uc2  PHILIPPINE PESO \par \par \uc2  The peso moved in a narrow range on the wrong side of the 50 to the dollar mark in early trade ahead of a widely expected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  decision to cut key overnight rates. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said most players were expecting the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cut its overnight borrowing and lending rates by another 50 basis points, after lowering them by a total of 100 basis points in the past two weeks. \par \par \uc2  But a deeper cut could result in volatile moves, they added. \par \par \uc2  "The market is already pricing in slowing down of overseas workers' remittances to normal pace by the end of this month. In this situation a bigger cut can result in a sharp fall of the peso," a dealer at a European bank in Manila said. \par \par \uc2  Remittances from overseas workers usually peak ahead of the Christmas holidays. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had hinted earlier in the week it was ready to trime overnight rates by another 50-100 basis points. The overnight borrowing rate now stands at 14 percent and the lending rate at 16.25 percent. \par \par \uc2  The rates were jacked up by four percentage points when the peso slid to 51.95 to the dollar at the height of the political scandal surrounding President Joseph Estrada. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market was likely to trade within a range of 50.00-50.50 on low volumes in the absence of any surprises as most players were on the sidelines ahead of the holidays and as Estrada's impeachment trial drags on. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  0121 GMT \par \par \uc2   Change on the day \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2     Latest bid     Prev Close    Pct