\par\par \b \uc2  Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  - Choppy waters lie ahead. \b0\par\par\uc2 1,920 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Dezember 2000\par 10:00\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Dec 14 (Reuters) - North Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were under pressure late on Thursday, but analysts said the rest of the region would soon feel the heat from slowing external demand. \par \par \uc2  Technology-driven stock markets in Taiwan and Korea slipped on the back of the Nasdaq's fall as investors fretted over the prospect of dwindling earnings in the technology sector. \par \par \uc2  Major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets shrugged off the official concession by Democrat presidential hopeful Al Gore and focused on the Japanese economy, which was showing signs of floundering. \par \par \uc2  Japan's Economic Planning Agency said the country's gross economic product during the July-September period fell 0.3 percent on quarter under an old calculation method. \par \par \uc2  A report in major Japanese newspaper Asahi Shimbun that said the agency was likely to make such a revision had been weighing on the yen earlier in the day. \par \par \uc2  The yen suffered another blow from data showing Japanese corporate bankruptcies rose for the 13th straight month in a row. \par \par \uc2  On top of the weaker yen and slowing U.S. economic growth, regional markets are also facing a slew of homegrown political and economic problems. \par \par \uc2  And analysts warn that with slowing external demand, Asia's domestic developments will take centre stage. "As global support for Asian economic growth subsides, the internal stories have moved front and center," said Merrill Lynch in its latest monthly publication on the regional economic outlook. \par \par \uc2  While Northeast Asia faces doubts over corporate and financial reforms, as well as political uncertainty in Taiwan's case, the Philippines, Thailand and Indonesia remain challenged by "the most difficult domestic economic and political stories," it added. \par \par \uc2  PHILIPPINE PESO \par \par \uc2  The peso slipped to the wrong side of 50 as unexpected local corporate demand for dollars emerged in a fairly illiquid market. \par \par \uc2  Dealers in Manila said the market had assumed most local companies had already filled their dollar requirements ahead of year-end. However, they said the dollar buy orders were small, and the peso stayed within a tight range of 49.95-50.15. \par \par \uc2  They also said the market was starting to price in another 50 basis point cut in the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's key overnight rates. The bank hinted earlier in the week it was ready to take the overnight rates down another 50-100 basis points, having cut a total of 100 basis points in the past two weeks. \par \par \uc2  While dealers said the peso was unlikely to react to a 50 basis point cut, it may buckle a little more if the bank lowers rates by 100 basis points. The overnight borrowing and lending rate were jacked up four percentage points when the peso slid to 51.95 to the dollar at the height of the political scandal surrounding President Joseph Estrada. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market was sluggish as most investors sit out on the sidelines ahead of the holidays and as Estrada's impeachment trial continues. \par \par \uc2  But problems in the economy continued to loom over markets. Finance Secretary Jose Pardo said earlier in the day the government had secured a $200 million loan to help bridge the country's huge budget deficit. He later retracted that comment, saying he had made a mistake. \par \par \uc2  The budget deficit for full year 2000 is expected to come in between 110 billion and 120 billion pesos against an earlier target of 62.5 billion pesos. \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar failed to extend gains beyond 1.7400 despite talk the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MAS) had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  overnight to curb the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent sharp rise. \par \par \uc2  A rise above 1.7400 was met promptly by dollar profit taking from corporate and interbank players, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "I guess the market is a tad long on dollars and players are unwinding their positions," said a dealer at an Asian bank in Singapore. "But I don't think market players will push (the Sing dollar) above 1.7350-1.7370 where the MAS was rumoured to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." \par \par \uc2  In the money market, the one-month forward rate was slightly firmer at -55.5/-54.5 while the six-month rate was quoted at -299/-294. \par \par \uc2  On the macro front, the government said the seasonally adjusted unemployment rate was 2.5 percent in September, down from 3.5 percent a year ago. \par \par \uc2  It said overall employment ws expected to grow in 2001 but at a slower pace, given that economic growth was likely to moderate next year. \par \par \uc2  KOREAN WON -2 \par \par \uc2  The won weakened past the 1,200 mark on a fall in the local equity market amid worries about tech-sector earnings, declines in the yen and on growing evidence of a slowing U.S. economy. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said some reprieve was likely from an expected overnight relief-rally on Wall Street after the five-week-long dispute over the U.S. presidential polls came to an end. \par \par \uc2  "There may be a relief-rally in U.S. stocks and that could provide some support...but judging by the reaction markets have given so far, it isn't going to be anything big or lasting," a chief dealer of a European bank in Singapore said. \par \par \uc2  Japan is Korea's closest export competitor while the U.S. is its biggest export market. The export sector, dominated by electronics, accounts for a large portion of Korean GDP. \par \par \uc2  The won was hit early after Korea's largest commercial bank Kookmin Bank said it had suspended merger talks with Housing and Commercial Bank in the face of worker protests and the threat of a strike by unions. \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the suspension highlighted impediments to Korea's corporate and financial restructuring drive. \par \par \uc2  Investors fear slowing growth and exports would test the government's resolve to go ahead with tough reforms already facing opposition from traditionally strong labour unions. \par \par \uc2  In the non-deliverable forward market, three-month NDFs firmed to 1,201/1,202 and six-month forwards to 1,201.5/1,202.5. Both were at 1,194/95 earlier in the day. \par \par \uc2  TAIWAN DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan dollar firmed on the back of heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers estimated that the bank spent around $250 million in the early part of the session to keep the local dollar above the 33.10 level. \par \par \uc2  A dealer at a U.S. bank in Singapore said the market was speculating that the bank's dollar selling activity had accounted for 50 to 70 percent of the daily turnover during the week. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. bank dealer said there was U.S. dollar buying interest on the NDF market in the three-month and six-month tenors, adding that buyers included "everybody who doesn't believe the CBC (\b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of China)." \par \par \uc2  Three-month Taiwan dollar NDFs were at 0.58/61 and the six-month stood at 0.77/80. \par \par \uc2  Craig Chan, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ING Barings in Hong Kong, said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had little choice but to let the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciate as Taiwan faces slowing exports and economic growth. \par \par \uc2  He said higher NDF rates reflected offshore investors' belief that the spot rate was not trading according to market forces, and they would ease when the spot Taiwan dollar was allowed to depreciate. \par \par \uc2  In addition to concerns over slowing growth, investor sentiment towards Taiwan has been battered by worries over increasing bad loans in the banking system. Analysts said the government had been unable to effectively address the problem. \par \par \uc2  Moreover, a weaker yen was also pressuring the local dollar. The cross rate between the yen and Taiwan dollar has crept up and stood at 3.3979 late Thursday. Analysts said the higher cross rate reinforced the view that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would allow the local dollar to depreciate. \par \par \uc2  THAI BAHT \par \par \uc2  The baht perked up, riding a liquidity squeeze and caution about the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's vigilance in enforcing \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading rules regarding baht credits to offshore players. \par \par \uc2  Baht funding costs ticked up in both offshore and onshore markets, with one-month offshore forwards quoted at 7.0/17.0 satang per dollar, up sharply from around -7.0/-3.0 satang late last week. \par \par \uc2  Onshore rates of the same tenor rose to -4.50/-4.0 satang from -5.50/-5.0 satang earlier. \par \par \uc2  The market is likely to focus on a bankruptcy court ruling on Thai Petrochemical Industry's $3.8 billion debt restructuring deal on Friday after repeated delays. \par \par \uc2  "If it's delayed again or if the court rules in favour of TPI, the baht will be hit. But the impact now might be limited given the market is being driven by year-end liquidity. People are also cautious about the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's close monitoring," said a dealer at a European bank in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  "But after the New Year holiday, when people return from vacation and when political uncertainty is heightened ahead of the elections, this factor will add heavily to the baht's downward bias," he said. \par \par \uc2  On the political front, Prime Minister Chuan Leekpai narrowed the gap on front-runner Thaksin Shinawatra ahead of the January 6 general election, but a large chunk of voters remain undecided. \par \par \uc2  The poll by ABAC, a Bangkok university, showed telecoms tycoon Thaksin still in front, backed by 28.2 percent of 4,177 Bangkok respondents compared with 29.9 percent in the previous poll in October. \par \par \uc2  INDONESIAN RUPIAH \par \par \uc2  The rupiah traded in a narrow range for most of the day with the downside capped by sporadic dollar-selling by state banks, suspected to be acting on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "It has been a very quiet day and even customer flows were very small and whatever pressure those flows created was absorbed by state banks," a dealer at a European bank in Jakarta said. \par \par \uc2  The dealer said state banks were spotted selling dollars at different levels from 9,440 to 9,410 and could have contributed over 10 percent of about $150 million turnover in the market. \par \par \uc2  "In the absence of most players, small amounts of dollar sales from state banks tend to force players to square long dollar positions," the dealer said. \par \par \uc2  He said with holidays approaching, the market was likely to get thinner and calmer by the day. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the market ignored a survey published by state-owned Danareksa Research Institute showing consumer confidence grew almost nine percent in Indonesia last month. \par \par \uc2  The monthly survey of 1,724 respondents said the consumer confidence index increased 8.8 percent to 56.8 from October, but pointed out that this did not reverse the downtrend in the index over the past six months. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  0859 GMT \par \par \uc2   Change on the day \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2     Latest bid     Prev Close    Pct Move \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Japan yen     112.62\tab  112.54\tab    -0.07\par  Sing dlr\tab 1.7387\tab  1.7369\tab    -0.10\par  Taiwan dlr    33.077\tab  33.081\tab    +0.01\par  Korean won   1202.00\tab 1191.40\tab    -0.88\par  Baht\tab      43.47\tab   43.59\tab    +0.28\par  Peso\tab      50.03\tab   49.90\tab    -0.26\par  Rupiah\tab  9425.00\tab 9435.00\tab    +0.11\par  Ringgit\tab  3.7995\tab  3.7995\tab    +0.00\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  Change on year\par \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \tab Latest     End prev yr     Pct Move\par  Japan yen     112.62\tab  102.21\tab    -9.24\par  Sing dlr\tab 1.7387\tab  1.6640\tab    -4.30\par  Taiwan dlr    33.077\tab  31.380\tab    -5.13\par  Korean won   1202.00\tab 1135.00\tab    -5.57\par  Baht\tab      43.47\tab   37.45\tab   -13.85\par  Peso\tab      50.03\tab   40.20\tab   -19.65\par  Rupiah\tab  9425.00\tab 6975.00\tab   -25.99\par  Ringgit\tab  3.7995\tab  3.7997\tab    +0.01.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dwce0a8i1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
