\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mixed Late; Won Back On Downward Track \b0\par\par\uc2  By Alan Yonan Jr. \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 699 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Dezember 2000\par 10:58\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The dollar advanced decisively against the South Korean won Thursday, while other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  finished mixed during regional trading hours. \par \par \uc2  Resisting the dollar's upward movement were the New Taiwan dollar, Indonesian rupiah and Thai baht, which strengthened modestly. \par \par \uc2  The won continued its downward slide after rallying against the dollar earlier in the week. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  closed above the psychologically important KRW1,200 level for the first time in six sessions. \par \par \uc2  A softening yen, lower local share prices and onshore dollar demand by banks and importers combined to pressure the won, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's uptrend against the won "remains intact," UBS Warburg said in a note to clients, noting that the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is in high demand by offshore participants in the market for non-deliverable forward contracts. \par \par \uc2  At the close of trading the dollar was quoted at 1,202.0 won, up from KRW1,193,8 at Wednesday's finish. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso weakened slightly, but finished within its trading range of recent weeks. \par \par \uc2  Although investor concerns about political stability in the Philippines has kept pressure on the peso, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to remain wedged between 49.3 and 50.5 per dollar until there is a clearer idea of the outcome in President Joseph Estrada's impeachment trial on corruption charges, DBS Bank said in a research report. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at PHP50.085 on the Philippine Dealing System, up from PHP49.975 and the end of trading Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  Philippine prosecutors and lawyers for Estrada Thursday grappled over the issue of whether to admit into evidence bank documents, including a PHP142 million check allegedly used to buy a mansion in Manila used by an Estrada mistress. \par \par \uc2  An expected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  cut in short-term interest rates of between 50 and 100 basis points Friday should have little impact on the peso, DBS said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar rose against its \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  counterpart after suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  in offshore markets overnight to restrain the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent appreciation. \par \par \uc2  Near the end of Asian trading, the U.S. dollar was quoted at S$1.7392, up from S$1.7374 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar opened stronger and short-covering by Singapore-based market participants and companies pushed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to an intraday high of S$1.7410 in early trading. \par \par \uc2  However, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  eased below the S$1.7400 level on subsequent selling by some U.S. investors. \par \par \uc2  The New Taiwan dollar benefited from \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  support and squaring of U.S. dollar long-positions by corporations. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar closed at NT$33.077, compared with NT$33.081 the day before. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  allowed the U.S. dollar to venture as high as NT$33.130 in the morning session before \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap it at NT$33.100 in afternoon trading. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold a total of US$150 million to support the local dollar, dealers estimated. \par \par \uc2  The Indonesian rupiah was supported by dollar selling by state banks, but dollar demand by local companies limited the rupiah's gains. \par \par \uc2  After falling to an intraday low of IDR9,400, the dollar closed at IDR9,430 in Asian trading, down slightly from IDR9,450 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  State banks, most notably Bank Mandiri (D.MAN) and Bank Rakyat Indonesia (P.RKI), actively sold the dollar. Dealers believed they were selling on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to ease the pressure on the rupiah. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht edged higher as the threat of an offshore swap squeeze on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept speculators out of the market. Against the Thai baht, the dollar was quoted at THB43.495 in late Asian trading, down from THB43.625 late Wednesday. \par \par \uc2  The dollar could get a boost against the baht Friday if Thai Petrochemical Industry PCL's (H.TPE) debt restructuring plan is further delayed or HSBC Holdings PLC's (HBC) bid to buy several nationalized banks fails. \par \par \uc2  -By Alan Yonan Jr., Dow Jones Newswires; 65 421-4859; alan.yonan@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dwce05ysp\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
