\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mostly Dn Late On Yen Fall; Won Hard Hit \b0\par\par\uc2  By Netty Ismail \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 774 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Dezember 2000\par 09:26\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Japanese yen's slump to its lowest level this year dealt a blow to most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  late Wednesday, with the South Korean won and the New Taiwan dollar feeling the brunt of the pain, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  While all the other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were weaker, the Indonesian rupiah bucked the trend, thanks to support from local state banks, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  A weaker-than-expected quarterly tankan survey of business sentiment by Bank of Japan and a U.S. Supreme Court ruling that all but sealed the election of George W. Bush as the next U.S. president sent the yen reeling to a year low of Y112.55 against the dollar, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0750 GMT (2:50 a.m. EST), the dollar was at Y112.43, up sharply from Y111.47 late Tuesday in New York. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. Supreme Court reversed a decision by the Florida Supreme Court to order a manual recount of thousands of ballots, likely sounding the death knell for Vice President Al Gore's presidential hopes. \par \par \uc2  "The weaker yen hit sentiment toward the Korean won and the Taiwan dollar," said Mansoor Mohi-uddin, a regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at UBS Warburg. \par \par \uc2  Strong gains on the Seoul bourse early Wednesday initially failed to counter the downward pressure on the won, which yielded to an offshore assault from the non-deliverable forward market, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  "Despite foreign equity purchases and a nearly 3% rally in the Kospi stock index, the Korean won attached itself to the yen and headed lower," said Peter Redward, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Deustche Bank. \par \par \uc2  He noted, however, that the dollar failed to make a sustainable break through the psychologically important KRW1,200 level. \par \par \uc2  After pulling back from around the 1,200 won mark on long dollar liquidation, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at KRW1193.30, up from KRW1,190.30 Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Taiwan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which accounted for close to half of the session's volume, helped alleviate the stress on the local dollar coming from the floundering yen, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  After languishing closer to NT$33.09-NT$33.10 against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  most of the day, the New Taiwan dollar ended slightly firmer as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped up its defense of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  toward the close of the session, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar ended at NT$33.081, down marginally Tuesday's close of NT$33.088. Trading volume was US$535.5 million. \par \par \uc2  The dollar's gains against the yen and the euro, combined with onshore dollar demand cast a shadow on the Singapore dollar and the Thai baht, dealers said. Offshore interest in the latter \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained sparse following the Thai government's recent crackdown on speculative trading in the baht. \par \par \uc2  Against the Singapore dollar, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at S$1.7374, up from S$1.7347 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  Tuesday around S$1.7320, to curb the local dollar's recent strength, also contributed to the reversal in the local dollar's recent ascent, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Mohi-uddin at UBS Warburg said this should keep the U.S. dollar supported above S$1.7300, since the MAS policy is for a gradual appreciation of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  DBS Bank said in a report Wednesday that the de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may find it "more acceptable in the current environment, to have the U.S. dollar near or even above S$1.7400, instead of S$1.7300." \par \par \uc2  The Singapore bank said that before the Singapore dollar can resume its recent rise, the euro must follow through on its recent rebound above US$0.88, the dollar needs to fall below Y110 and sentiment must improve for "politically troubled" \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as the Indonesian rupiah and the Philippine peso. \par \par \uc2  In the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, the dollar edged up to 43.625 baht, from THB43.520 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso closed lower in thin trade, with the market cautiously tracking President Joseph Estrada's impeachment trial. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at PHP49.975 on the Philippine Dealing System, up from PHP49.850 at the previous close. \par \par \uc2  Resisting the downward pressure from its regional peers, the rupiah was stronger, thanks to dollar selling by state banks, believed to be acting on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The dollar was at 9,440 rupiah, down from IDR9,510 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  -By Netty Ismail, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-421-4807; netty.ismail@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dwcd05zkj\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
