\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Dn Late But Off Low; Tracks Weak Regionals \b0\par\par\uc2 384 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 November 2000\par 09:28\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar was lower against its U.S. counterpart late Thursday, tracking weaker Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but it was off its intraday low, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0808 GMT (3:08 a.m. EST), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.7573, up from S$1.7564 late Wednesday in Asia. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar opened higher, and hit an intraday high of S$1.7590 midmorning on buying by European and Japanese names. Local banks started selling U.S. dollars on behalf of corporate clients at this level, forcing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to partially surrender its gains. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said Thursday's data showing a higher-than-expected 4% on-year growth in bank lending in Singapore also helped bolster sentiment toward the Singapore dollar. \par \par \uc2  But local banks' selling U.S. dollars was the main reason for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's partial retreat in the afternoon, a dealer said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said the U.S. dollar has been well supported at around S$1.7570, with stronger near-term support at S$1.7550. On the upside, fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  are expected to cap the U.S. dollar at around S$1.7600-20. \par \par \uc2  On the bond market, prices of Singapore Government Securities rose sharply in early trade, tracking strong overnight gains in U.S. Treasurys. \par \par \uc2  Light selling emerged in the afternoon as market participants adjusted their portfolio positions, but prices ended significantly higher on the day. \par \par \uc2  Bond yields fell by three to five basis points. The yield on the 10-year bond ended five basis points lower at 4.16%. The seven-year yield bid fell four basis points to 3.86%. The five-year yield ended three basis points lower at 3.48%. \par \par \uc2  In the money market, term rates were mostly flat. The overnight rate edged slightly higher, but still within its normal trading range. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                   Late Thursday      Late Wednesday\par                   bid      offer     bid      offer\par Overnight         2.3750%  2.6250%   2.0000%  1.8750%\par 3 Month           2.6875%  2.8125%   2.6875%  2.8125%\par 1 Year            2.8125%  2.9375%   2.8125%  2.9375%\par -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; (+65) 421-4809;\par amit.prakash@dowjones.com\par -0- 30/11/00 08-39G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dwbu05s0r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
