\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Lower Late In Choppy Trade;Bonds End Higher \b0\par\par\uc2 387 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 November 2000\par 09:59\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar was weaker against its U.S. counterpart late Friday, broadly mirroring the movements of the Thai baht in a rather choppy session, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0847 GMT (3:47 a.m. EST), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.7589, up from S$1.7565 late Thursday in Asia. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar opened higher and hit S$1.7592, its highest level since Sept. 4, 1998, in tandem with its early rise against the Thai baht to an intraday high of THB43.97. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar's rise to S$1.7592 made market participants jittery about possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 . And the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's subsequent fall to THB43.47 prompted banks to sell U.S. dollars for Singapore dollars. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar fell to an intraday low of S$1.7558, which attracted large local companies to start buying the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Dealers expect \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears to cap the U.S. dollar around S$1.7600 in the near term, with strong support around S$1.7540. \par \par \uc2  On the bond market, Singapore Government Securities ended higher on good demand, particularly for the longer-dated maturities, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Bond yields were lower, with the 10-year yield ending two basis points lower at 4.28%, while the seven-year yield fell three basis points to 3.99%. The five-year yield ended three basis points lower at 3.60%. \par \par \uc2  In the money market, the overnight offered rate rose to a high of 3.25% midmorning, from around 2% late Thursday, on demand for funds ahead of the weekend. However, it fell to 1.6250% in late afternoon trading as lenders flooded the market with surplus cash. \par \par \uc2  Term rates remained steady at the higher levels reach earlier this week on the back of weaker regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                   Late Friday        Late Thursday\par                   bid      offer     bid      offer\par Overnight         1.5000%  1.6250%   1.8750%  2.0000%\par 3 Month           2.6875%  2.8125%   2.5625%  2.6875%\par 1 Year            2.8125%  2.9375%   2.7500%  2.8750%\par -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; (+65) 421-4809;\par amit.prakash@dowjones.com\par -0- 24/11/00 08-59G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dwbo05lgq\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
