\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Slightly Higher Late; Mirrors Baht Moves \b0\par\par\uc2 379 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 November 2000\par 09:15\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar was slightly higher against the U.S. dollar late Thursday, but off its intraday high on a weakening baht and North Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0745 GMT (2:45 a.m. EST), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.7565, down from S$1.7570 late Wednesday in Asia. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar opened lower, around S$1.7525, in line with its overnight losses against the baht and the Singapore dollar in New York. \par \par \uc2  Tight liquidity had caused the U.S. dollar to fall sharply against the baht in New York, which caused the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to fall to a low of S$1.7515 against the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Liquidity returned to the U.S. dollar/baht market when Asian markets reopened Thursday and the U.S. dollar drifted higher, hitting a high of 43.905 baht in late afternoon trading. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar's rebound against the baht, as well as its gain against the South Korean won and the New Taiwan dollar, pushed it higher against the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  However, fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  are expected to cap the U.S. dollar around S$1.7575-80. On the downside, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to be well supported at S$1.7520. \par \par \uc2  On the bonds market, Singapore Government Securities ended nearly flat amid very light trading ahead of the results, later Thursday, of an auction for S$2 billion worth of two-year government bonds. \par \par \uc2  Bond yields were flat, with the 10-year yielding 4.30%, seven-year 4.02%, five-year 3.63% and the two-year 2.84%. \par \par \uc2  In the money market, the overnight rate eased as S$1.3 billion from maturing treasury bills flowed into the banking system. \par \par \uc2  However, the term rates rose following a further weakening of regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                   Late Thursday      Late Wednesday\par                   bid      offer     bid      offer\par Overnight         1.8750%  2.0000%   2.2500%  2.3750%\par 3 Month           2.6875%  2.8125%   2.5625%  2.6875%\par 1 Year            2.8125%  2.9375%   2.7500%  2.8750%\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; (+65) 421-4809;\par amit.prakash@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dwbn05o54\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
