\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mostly Weaker, Led By NT Dlr Drop \b0\par\par\uc2  By Miyako Takebe \par \uc2 973 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Oktober 2000\par 11:19\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    TOKYO -(Dow Jones)- Most Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  were weaker late Wednesday,\par with the New Taiwan dollar dropping to a new 16-month low late in the\par session after the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  withdrew active support for the local\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  Bucking the downtrend was the Indonesian rupiah, which rose slightly after sovereign donors agreed to provide Jakarta with US$4.8 billion in budget aid for 2001 to plug a fiscal deficit expected to amount to 3.7% of gross domestic product. \par \par \uc2  After spending an estimated US$800 million during the day to defend the New Taiwan dollar, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  of China - the local \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  - late in the session loosened its grip on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , allowing it to fall briefly to NT$32.500 against the U.S. dollar. Traders said the move appeared to signal a shift by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to easier monetary conditions in a bid to stave off a deepening liquidity crunch. A massive outflow of capital from the equities market has tightened liquidity for some sectors of the economy. \par \par \uc2  "It (the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) had to do something soon or the economy would likely have landed very, very hard," said a U.S. bank trader in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  The Taiwan Index of stock prices, or Taiex, plunged 4.7% Wednesday to 5,432.23, its lowest close in four and a half years. The index is down 36% so far this year and has plummeted 47% from its high for the year set in February. \par \par \uc2  Simmering political concerns in several Asian nations and a savage drop overnight in prices of U.S. semiconductor issues combined to undermine many stock markets in the region Wednesday. The Philadelphia Stock Exchange's semiconductor index fell 9.4% in a reaction to fresh profit warnings. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar ended domestic trade in Taipei at NT$32.383, up from NT$32.260 a day earlier and marking its highest close since June 1999. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean won slid to its lowest levels since May in early trading, although it managed to pare those losses after the stock market staged a spectacular rebound late in the day following the announcement of stock-supportive measures by the South Korean government. \par \par \uc2  The dollar initially rose as high as KRW1,141.50, its highest level since May 24, as the Korea Stock Exchange buckled at the outset of trade, shedding close to 5% during the morning. But the South Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rebounded smartly at the end of the day, pushing the dollar down to KRW1,136.7 at the close. That was still a higher close for the dollar compared with KRW1,133.20 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  The South Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economy said that it will support the stock market by providing 1 trillion won in funds to local investment-trust companies through Seoul Guarantee Insurance Co. The government also said it will ease regulations on corporate stock buybacks to help companies support their stock prices. \par \par \uc2  Korea's stock index ended the day 0.3% higher. \par \par \uc2  In Manila, the Philippine peso suffered in response to news that the International Monetary Fund had decided to cancel $300 million in loans to the nation because of Manila's failure to meet fiscal targets attached as conditions for the loan program. That, coupled with a fresh bout of political jitters after opposition politicians submitted an impeachment resolution against President Joseph Estrada, pushed the dollar as high as PHP48.700. \par \par \uc2  The peso later regained some of those losses, with the dollar closing at 48.570 pesos, up from PHP48.300 at the previous close. \par \par \uc2  The impeachment complaint against Estrada accused him of accepting millions of dollars in bribes from gambling operators. \par \par \uc2  "We have yet to see an easing of the political concern. It deepens day by day, mainly due to uncertainties," said a trader from another local bank. \par \par \uc2  "The political scandal is only adding to the gridlock" over the peso, which continues to suffer from concerns over how the government will make up for its lack of funds to cover the gaping fiscal deficit, said Desmond Supple, head of research at Barclays Capital in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar slipped late Wednesday as it succumbed to the weakness of its regional counterparts, but the U.S. dollar remained capped below S$1.7570 as market participants were wary of provoking another round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was said to have defended the Singapore dollar through several banks between S$1.7560 and S$1.7580 against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  last Friday. \par \par \uc2  In late trading, the dollar was at S$1.7560, up from S$1.7533 late Tuesday in Asia. \par \par \uc2  In Bangkok, the dollar rose sharply against the baht after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  indicated it remains unconcerned about the weakening of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Bank of Thailand Governor Chatu Mongol Sonakul said that the current level of the baht is good for exports and isn't worrisome, as the amount of Thai private sector foreign debt is "very low." \par \par \uc2  "Therefore the low baht will not increase the debt burden of the private sector," he said, adding that there is no need for the BOT to raise benchmark interest rates. \par \par \uc2  Late in the day, the dollar was trading at 43.705 baht, up from 43.365 baht a day earlier. \par \par \uc2  Bucking the overall trend, news that international donors meeting in Tokyo had agreed to go through with the fresh loans to Jakarta - although widely expected - buoyed the rupiah. The dollar stood at 8,875 rupiah in late Asian trading, down from IDR8,895 late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; miyako.takebe@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dwai051gt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
