\par\par \b \uc2  MAS Likely To Tolerate Some Singapore Dlr Weakness \b0\par\par\uc2  By Netty Ismail \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 785 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Oktober 2000\par 11:15\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore will likely tolerate some further weakening of the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as it maintains a neutral policy stance for the rest of the year, according to analysts. \par \par \uc2  Nonetheless, traders are uneasy about pushing the Singapore dollar much lower, as it hovers near the levels that provoked a suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the MAS last week. \par \par \uc2  The MAS, which rarely comments on its actions in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, would likely step in to smooth out any excessive movements in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar, the analysts say, but any \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  isn't expected to be aggressive. \par \par \uc2  "As long as inflation is contained, they will let it (the Singapore dollar) go," says an economist at an Asian bank, who expects an inflation rate of 1.6% this year. The MAS expects inflation to remain below 2% for the year. \par \par \uc2  The MAS uses the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , rather than interest rates, as its primary monetary-policy tool in seeking to maintain brisk growth and low inflation. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar is guided within an undisclosed trading band against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of the country's major trading partners and has remained relatively stable against these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Despite having signaled its preference for a relatively stronger Singapore dollar on a trade-weighted basis in the coming year, the MAS won't be in a rush to shift to a tightening bias this year, analyst say. \par \par \uc2  "We don't really see any significant pressure on the authority to shift to a tightening stance" for the rest of the year, says David Simmonds, a regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Salomon Smith Barney/Citibank. \par \par \uc2  Higher oil prices, the increasingly cloudy prospects of the electronics industry and political instability in some Southeast Asian countries will continue to cast a shadow over other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which in turn will weigh on the Singapore dollar, analysts say. \par \par \uc2  And the pressure from North Asia is also building up. The South Korean won and the New Taiwan dollar, which earlier this year stood out as bastions of stability, have recently buckled under the pressure of drooping local equity prices and a surge in oil prices, analysts say. \par \par \uc2  The euro too remains shaky after hitting a record low of $0.8438 last month. \par \par \uc2  "The MAS will have to wait for the dust to settle first before they do anything," says the economist at an Asian bank. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Fears Of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Keep US Dlr Below S$1.7570\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Fears of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the MAS have kept the U.S. dollar below\par S$1.7570 Wednesday, the level that traders speculate could provoke such\par a move.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Around 0830 GMT, the U.S. dollar was at S$1.7560, up from S$1.7533\par late Tuesday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The MAS was suspected of having defended the Singapore dollar through\par several banks last Friday above S$1.7560 against the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , as\par the local unit fell to its lowest level in almost 25 months.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Mansoor Mohi-uddin, a regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at UBS Warburg,\par says he expects further \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the MAS if the U.S. dollar rises\par to S$1.7578, the high last Friday before the reported \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 \par supported the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Market participants have been fearful of testing the resolve of the\par MAS after it said in late July that it was prepared to allow for a\par gradual and modest appreciation of the Singapore dollar against the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of its major trading partners over the coming year to ward\par off incipient inflationary pressures.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Since then, the Singapore dollar has appreciated about 1.2% relative\par to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of its top-12 major trading partners, as the euro and\par other Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  experienced sharp falls against the U.S. dollar,\par says the Asian bank economist.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Against the U.S. dollar alone, however, the Singapore dollar has\par weakened about 0.6% since the end of July.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Some analysts say it will only be a matter of time before the\par Singapore dollar slides to S$1.7600 against its U.S. counterpart, which\par was last tested in September 1998, at the height of the regional\par financial crisis.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par And if the MAS continues to sit on the sidelines, the Singapore dollar\par will hit this level "very soon," says the Asian bank economist.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Looking farther ahead, Simmonds of Salomon Smith Barney/Citibank\par expects the U.S. dollar to trade around S$1.7500 in a month's time and\par around S$1.7400 and S$1.7500 in the next three months, after the bank\par lowered its forecasts on the Singapore dollar last week.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Netty Ismail, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-421-4807;\par netty.ismail@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dwai051g1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
