\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS-Philippine peso no Pied Piper for regional  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 . \b0\par\par\uc2  By Sonali Desai \par \uc2 710 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Oktober 2000\par 08:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Oct 17 (Reuters) - With its deepening political crisis and floundering markets, the Philippines is putting up a tough fight for Indonesia's ranking as the least sought-after investor destination in Asia. \par \par \uc2  The peso's almost daily slide to new lows has soured already poor sentiment for other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent weeks, but analysts see little basis for a widespread knock-on impact. \par \par \uc2  However, with no shortage of domestic worries and the grim backdrop of high oil prices and jittery U.S. stocks, investors have ample reason to stay away from Asia generally, they say. \par \par \uc2  "These \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  obviously are still correlated. But people are not saying that if the Philippine peso goes to 50, by default the baht has to go to 46," said Steve Brice, treasury economist at Standard Chartered. \par \par \uc2  "That may actually happen, but one doesn't lead to the other. There are domestic factors in Thailand that would lead to the baht weakening, and domestic factors in the Philippines." \par \par \uc2  The peso was hovering around 48.44/50 to the dollar at 0530 GMT, off Monday's all-time low of 49.00, as the threat of another massive interest rate hike held the market in check. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Tuesday it had not yet decided on a further tightening after raising overnight rates by 400 basis points and hiking bank reserve requirements twice in the past two weeks. \par \par \uc2  FUTILE EFFORTS? \par \par \uc2  Higher rates and repeated \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  have done little to support the peso as jitters about President Joseph Estrada's gambling payoff scandal stoke pessimism about an ever-expanding fiscal deficit and slowing economy. \par \par \uc2  Estrada, facing calls for his resignation from the predominantly Catholic country's powerful clergy, has said he was ready to face impeachment action over the scandal. \par \par \uc2  Manila markets continue to head south as the saga unfolds, and analysts say the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's efforts to shore up the peso have assumed an increasingly desperate tone. \par \par \uc2  "We think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has misjudged market sentiment... Just by imposing higher interest rates, what little foreign investor interest there is around the region may be scared away from the Philippines," said Stanchart's Brice. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say the peso's weakness is being driven by domestic corporate and retail buying of dollars and higher interest rates are only likely to hurt the fragile economy. \par \par \uc2  A number of banks have slashed their end-year peso forecasts in the wake of its sharp drop against the dollar and no sign of a let-up in the gloom. \par \par \uc2  Barclays Capital and JP Morgan are among the more bearish, with an end-2000 forecast of 52. Citibank expects the peso to reach 50.5 on a one-month horizon and Stanchart is looking at 50 over the same period. \par \par \uc2  BAHT EXPOSED \par \par \uc2  So far, the Thai baht has tended to be most susceptible to weakness in the peso as traders link the two \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  due to similarities in their economies. \par \par \uc2  The baht was quoted at 43.33/36 at 0530 GMT, well off Friday's 28-month low of 43.68. \par \par \uc2  But apart from some shared difficulties on the political front, large fiscal deficits and similar status on the technology chain, the Thai economy is more than twice the size of the Philippines in terms of gross domestic product. \par \par \uc2  And Thailand has more than its own share of problems, with elections looming and investors still concerned about the slow pace of financial sector restructuring. \par \par \uc2  Low interest rates and the Thai \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's hands-off exchange rate policy have also made the baht a prime funding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts say. \par \par \uc2  "What we have seen in the last several weeks are investors who want to have some short exposure to the region are looking around and finding that negative carries are very high in Indonesia and the Philippines," said Rebecca Patterson, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan. \par \par \uc2  "The baht is the one outlier. You have lower yield and you have a \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  that has a track record of not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  nearly as much as the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 . So I think investors are going to continue using the baht as their vehicle to short," she said. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010822dwah043k7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
