\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mixed Late; 6th Straight Loss For Peso \b0\par\par\uc2 651 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Oktober 2000\par 12:31\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  turned in a mixed performance Monday, with threats of a presidential impeachment in the Philippines continuing to keep the peso on the defensive. \par \par \uc2  The peso slumped for the sixth straight session as President Joseph Estrada braced for possible impeachment proceedings amid allegations he received millions of dollars in gambling kickbacks. \par \par \uc2  Elsewhere, the Singapore dollar, Thai baht and Indonesian rupiah avoided the peso downdraft and posted marginal gains. In North Asia, the South Korean won strengthened, while falling stock prices pushed the New Taiwan dollar to a 14-month low. \par \par \uc2  Against the peso, the dollar soared to an intraday high of PHP49.000 in early trading before the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The dollar gave up some of its gains, but still managed to close at a new record high of PHP48.670, up from 48.500 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  Estrada, who has denied receiving "even a cent" from illegal gambling, Monday challenged his political opponents to "go ahead with their impeachment." \par \par \uc2  The House of Representatives must investigate any impeachment charges against Estrada. If it finds the charges substantial, it sends them to the Senate, which conducts a trial and decides a verdict. \par \par \uc2  The peso will continue to face selling pressure in the fourth quarter due to the corruption saga, potentially losing another 2% of its value, DBS Bank said in a note to clients. \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht was helped by improving sentiment for regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and forecasts that third quarter earnings at Thai banks will be the best since the Asian financial crisis, traders said. \par \par \uc2  Near the end of Asian trading, the dollar was quoted at THB43.195, down from THB43.575 late Friday. \par \par \uc2  Still, overall market sentiment toward the baht will remain bearish due to political risks associated with general elections, expected in December or early January. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     The New Taiwan dollar tumbled to 14-month low against the U.S.\par dollar as share prices on the Taipei exchange fell to a 20-month low\par in late trading.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The U.S. dollar closed at NT$31.990, up from the previous close of\par NT$31.920.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Weighted Price Index of the Taiwan Stock Exchange fell 4.2% as\par investors took profit on the bourse's early gains and offloaded tech\par shares.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The rupiah rebounded ahead of a meeting of Indonesia's international\par donors this week in Tokyo. Indonesia is seeking $4.8 billion in new\par funding in 2001 to help bridge its widening budget gap.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Indonesian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also got a lift from the strengthening\par Singapore dollar and Thai baht, dealers said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Late in Asia the dollar was quoted at IDR8,875, down from IDR8,922\par late Friday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dealers said offshore participants unwound their dollar positions, but\par dollar bids from state banks prevented the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from falling\par sharply.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Singapore dollar was buoyed by the strength in other regional\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and by improved sentiment following the Nasdaq's Friday\par rebound.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Market players also remained wary of testing the resolve of the\par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which is suspected of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to\par support the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Friday after the U.S. dollar rose to the\par S$1.7560-S$1.7580 range.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The U.S. dollar was at S$1.7515, in late Asian trading down from\par S$1.7545 late Friday.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The South Korean won tracked the Seoul stock market higher.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The dollar finished at 1,127.40 won, down from Friday's close of\par KRW1,128.60.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par After ending Friday at its lowest finish since February 1999, the\par benchmark Korea Composite Stock Price Index, rose 25.50 points, or\par 4.86%, to end Monday at 550.10, following a rebound Friday in the Nasdaq\par Composite Index and a decline in crude-oil prices.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par -By Alan Yonan Jr., Dow Jones Newswires; 65 421-4859;\par alan.yonan@dowjones.com\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dwag04yx3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
