\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore Dlr Up Late On Baht, Nasdaq Rebound \b0\par\par\uc2 467 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Oktober 2000\par 11:17\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The Singapore dollar was stronger late Monday, finding reprieve from a firmer Thai baht and a rebound on Nasdaq last Friday, as tensions in the Middle East appeared to ease, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Suspected moves by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore in defending the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  last Friday, at around S$1.7560 to S$1.7580 against the U.S. dollar, also made market participants wary of pushing the Singapore dollar lower, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  A dealer at a U.S. bank said the MAS has probably drawn a line at S$1.7560. \par \par \uc2  Around 0830 GMT (4:30 a.m. EDT) Monday, the U.S. dollar was at S$1.7515, down from S$1.7545 late Friday in Asia. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar had opened at an intraday high of S$1.7535 at the start of Asian trading, but it pulled back to as low as S$1.7505 as the Thai baht rebounded, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  But the Philippine peso's decline to yet another historic low against the U.S. dollar helped the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  recover some of its lost ground to around S$1.7520, where it lingered most of the day, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  skidded to a new record low of PHP49 against the U.S. dollar Monday, amid concerns over President Joseph Estrada's hold on power following accusations that he received kickbacks from illegal gambling \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Despite reports of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last Friday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely allow a moderate weakness in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  if regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  continue to slide, analysts said. The Singapore dollar has been relatively stable compared with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of its major trading partners, they added. \par \par \uc2  "If the regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weaken, there is probably some room to tolerate some Singapore dollar weakness, but not excessively," said Sim Moh Siong, an economist at Salomon Smith Barney/Citibank. \par \par \uc2  The government's policy is "probably in neutral mode, with bias towards tightening next year," he added. \par \par \uc2  Yields on benchmark Singapore government bonds were mostly unchanged from late Friday's levels. \par \par \uc2  The yield on the 10-year bond was quoted at 4.41%, unchanged from Friday, while the seven-year paper was one basis point lower at 4.05%. \par \par \uc2  In the interbank market, bids for the overnight rate fell rose to 2.3750%, from 2.7500% on Friday. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Trading in the money market was subdued amid a dearth of market-moving\par news, dealers said.\par                   Late Monday       Late Friday\par                   bid     offer     bid     offer\par Overnight         2.3750% 2.5000%   2.7500% 2.8750%\par 3 Month           2.4375% 2.5625%   2.4375% 2.5625%\par -By Netty Ismail, Dow Jones Newswires; 65/421-4809;\par netty.ismail@dowjones.com\par -0- 16/10/00 09-17G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dwag04yoa\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
