\par\par \b \uc2  MAS seen allowing Sing easing despite buying talk. \b0\par\par\uc2 564 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Oktober 2000\par 11:08\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Oct 13 (Reuters) - The Singapore dollar's rise from near 25-month lows on Friday sparked talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but analysts said regardless of whether it had happened, authorities would tolerate a lower local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "They are not averse to a slightly weaker Sing at this time. They just don't want a one-way trade," said Vasan Shridharan, treasury economist at Standard Chartered. \par \par \uc2  Dealers said local banks were seen selling the U.S. dollar near a high of S$1.7575, spurring talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  The local bank sales sent the dollar to near S$1.7515 and it was quoted at S$1.7523 late on Friday. \par \par \uc2  "Given the current (weak regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) environment, I believe they probably had to come in," said Song Seng Wun, economist at GK Goh Securities. \par \par \uc2  "We ought to have seen the dollar at least at S$1.76," he said, noting falls by the Philippine peso, Thai baht and Indonesian rupiah. \par \par \uc2  The three are believed to be in an undisclosed basket of trade-weighted \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  the MAS uses to manage the exchange rate. The U.S. unit makes up some 20 percent of the basket. \par \par \uc2  The 1.76 level to the dollar was last seen during the Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis in September 1998. \par \par \uc2  MAS SMOOTHS \par \par \uc2  A MAS spokeswoman said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does not comment on market speculation, but the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said it occasionally does \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to smooth out excessive volatility. \par \par \uc2  Song said the MAS could allow the Sing dollar to fall to 1.80 to the U.S. unit if declines in regional counterparts continued. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar touched an Asian crisis low of 1.8150. Of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  affected, it is among the closest to its nadir on a percentage basis. \par \par \uc2  Analysts say the MAS would see limited benefit in trying to aggressively push the Sing dollar higher and noted its relative inaction since July, when it said it was prepared to allow a gradual appreciation. \par \par \uc2  The comments gave an upward jolt to the Singapore dollar that has subsequently been erased. \par \par \uc2  Shridharan, said the weakening occurred because authorities were less worried about inflationary pressure now than in July, while the spectre of a global slowdown had become more menacing. \par \par \uc2  "If you look at recent comments, they don't reckon oil as big a problem as other nations," he said. \par \par \uc2  "The possibility of a global slowdown has been a concern and that possibility has increased." \par \par \uc2  He said the Sing's upside was limited to near 1.7480. \par \par \uc2  GROWTH NOT AN ISSUE \par \par \uc2  Singapore's robust economic growth outlook, officially pegged at near nine percent in 2000 and 5-7 percent next year, is also not forcing the MAS' hand. \par \par \uc2  "Growth is not the primary consideration in monetary policy," said P.K. Basu, economist at Credit Suisse First Boston. \par \par \uc2  "It's basically within the range they want it to be... (The Sing's) remained fairly stable against the trade-weighted basket." \par \par \uc2  But Basu said inflation would be an issue for the MAS and a tightening could be expected if the consumer price index continued to see higher month-on-month growth. \par \par \uc2  CPI rose 0.5 percent month-on-month in August and in July. \par \par \uc2  He pegged a year-end rate of 1.72 to the U.S. dollar. \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010822dwad047vn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
