\par\par \b \uc2  Singapore ST Index Open 3.3% Lower On Wall St Losses \b0\par\par\uc2 622 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 Oktober 2000\par 03:01\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The shock wave from the violence in the Middle East that ravaged Wall Street is sweeping through Asia, including Singapore, Friday. \par \par \uc2  In early trading, the Straits Times Index broke past the 1800-point support level and fell 3.6%, or 66.86 points, to 1798.99. \par \par \uc2  The DBS-50 index, which gives greater weighting to the largest stocks and excludes foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issues, was down 3.25%, or 19.96 points, to 612.76. \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market early Friday through a number of local banks to buoy the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has been dragged down by weakness in the baht and peso, traders said. \par \par \uc2  They said the de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped into the market and bought Singapore dollars around the S$1.7560/U.S. dollar level, pushing the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  up to around S$1.7525. \par \par \uc2  In the bond markets, the yield on the benchmark government 10-year bonds and the seven-year bonds are also higher at the open, dealer said. The 10-year was bid at 4.43% while the yield on the seven-year was at 4.07% from 4.05% Thursday. \par \par \uc2  The turmoil was expected. The killing of two Israeli soldiers by a Palestinian mob Thursday led to a forceful retaliatory attack by Israeli forces on Palestinian targets. \par \par \uc2  Around the same time, an explosion ripped a gaping hole in the 9,100-ton U.S. Navy destroyer USS Cole, killing six members of the crew, injuring 35, leaving 11 missing, and putting Yemen at the center of what appeared to be a terrorist plot against the U.S. \par \par \uc2  Wall Street - where sentiment was already fragile - fell sharply. The Dow Jones Industrial Average closed 3.6% lower at 10034.58, and the Nasdaq shed another 3% to 3074.68. Crude oil prices rose to a 10-year high on fears of a disruption in supplies as winter approaches in the northern hemisphere. \par \par \uc2  In Asia-Pacific trading, the Nikkei 225 was down 2% and South Korea's Kospi index plunged 6% at one point. \par \par \uc2  In Singapore, the STI briefly fell below the 1800-point level. At 0128 GMT it was at 1813.20, down 2.8%. \par \par \uc2  Selling in Singapore was across the board. A total of 317 stocks were down, 20 were up, and 45 were unchanged. \par \par \uc2  Singapore Airlines, which has one of the strongest fuel hedging policies of any airline, was down 4.2%, or 70 cents, to S$15.70, on trade of 400,000 shares. \par \par \uc2  Among technology stocks, Venture Manufacturing was down 70 cents, or 4.8%, to S$14, on trade of 478,000 shares. Chartered Semiconductor shares plunged 8.3%, or 65 cents, to S$7.20, on trade of 2.1 million shares. \par \par \uc2  Among banks, DBS Group Holdings was down 2.1%, or 40 cents, to S$18.30, on trade of 1 million shares. United Overseas Bank shed 40 cents, or 3.3%, to S$11.90, on trade of 379,000 shares. \par \par \uc2  However, Singapore Technology Engineering was up 2%, or 5 cents, to S$2.51, on trade of 259,000 shares. \par \par \uc2  The government-linked defense conglomerate reported after market close Thursday a net profit of S$201.3 million for the nine months to Sept. 30, up 39% on-year. \par \par \uc2  Oil-related stocks also gained. Singapore Petroleum Co. was up 6.5 cents, or 7.8%, to S$0.90, on trade of 260,000, while Keppel Fels Energy was up one cent to S$1.17, on trade of 192,000 shares. \par \par \uc2  