\par\par \b \uc2  Asian  \highlight22\uc2 Currencies \highlight\uc2  Mostly Down Late; Peso Hits Record Low \b0\par\par\uc2  By Netty Ismail \par \uc2  Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par \uc2 852 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Oktober 2000\par 11:38\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- Most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened late Tuesday, with the Philippine peso feeling the brunt of the pain as it tumbled to a fresh record low. \par \par \uc2  Renewed strength in crude oil prices overnight - fueled by mounting concerns over escalating strife in the Middle East and a cold snap that hit the U.S. Northeast - combined with the languid performance on Asian bourses to push the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Bucking the downtrend were the Singapore dollar and the Indonesian rupiah. \par \par \uc2  The rupiah, which benefited from spiking oil prices, stayed mostly flat throughout the day, while the Singapore dollar recovered most of its early losses by late in the day, climbing to around Monday's levels by late trade. \par \par \uc2  The Philippine peso moved into uncharted waters again for the second time in a week, skidding to its lowest level in history against the U.S. dollar, on the region's weakness and heightened political anxieties. \par \par \uc2  The dollar closed at 46.890 pesos on the Philippine Dealing System, up from Monday's close of PHP46.550. \par \par \uc2  The dollar hit an intraday high of PHP46.900. \par \par \uc2  Fears of potential impeachment proceedings against President Joseph Estrada, following allegations that he and his family received kickbacks from illegal gambling \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , continued to unnerve market participants, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Governor of northern Ilocos Sur province Luis Singson, Monday alleged Estrada received more than PHP400 million from illegal gambling as well as PHP130 million from his province's tax revenues. \par \par \uc2  The allegations are the most serious yet against Estrada, who assumed the presidency in 1998, and have triggered discussions of impeachment proceedings. \par \par \uc2  "The attempt to get impeachment proceedings initiated will create far more political risks," said Simon Flint, an analyst at Bank of America. \par \par \uc2  With the government already burdened by a budget deficit that has already exceeded its yearly target and a prolonged insurgency in the south, it will only be a matter of time before the dollar breaks above PHP47, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  "We're pretty bearish on the peso," Flint said. "It's a question of whether they (the authorities) can short-circuit the overwhelmingly negative market sentiment and negative political fundamentals." \par \par \uc2  News that Philippine oil companies will be able to acquire up to $300 million a month under a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk protection program, aimed at easing the recent heavy dollar demand which has depressed the peso, has failed to provide any reprieve for the beleaguered \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  Dealers said they were disappointed that users of the facility will have to pay market rates for their funds, and won't receive any subsidy from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \par \par \uc2  "It turned out to be a major letdown," a U.S. bank dealer said. "Initially, people thought they could take up their hedging activity at a subsidized rate, but it turned out that they could only take it at the market rate." \par \par \uc2  The Thai baht continued to fall on higher oil prices, a weak euro and rising political tensions ahead of the country's general elections, expected later this year, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  Around 0935 GMT (5:35 a.m. EDT), the dollar was at 42.875 baht, up from THB42.585 late Monday. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar was unaffected by robust economic data released earlier in the day, and yielded to the pressure from its regional counterparts and the weak euro, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  The Ministry for Trade and Industry Tuesday raised its economic growth forecast for 2000 to around 9%, from a range of 7.5% to 8.5% previously. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar climbed to as high as S$1.7525 before retreating late in the day to S$1.7502, little changed from S$1.7498 late Monday. \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar's sharp intraday rebound had initially raised speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 . A local bank, which normally \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the MAS, was selling the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  around S$1.7520, dealers said. \par \par \uc2  But dealers said the selling was just part of normal trading activity. \par \par \uc2  With the U.S. dollar trading above the S$1.7500 level, the de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may tolerate a slight weakness in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , an economist at a local bank suggested. \par \par \uc2  "Inflation remains extremely benign," the economist said. \par \par \uc2  Also, despite weakness against the U.S. dollar, the Singapore dollar has appreciated by about 1.5% against the \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  of its major trading partners since late July, when the de facto \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  signaled its preference for a stronger local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the economist said. \par \par \uc2  He added that the U.S. dollar will likely trade between S$1.7300 and S$1.7600 for the rest of the year, "with bias toward the upside." \par \par \uc2  In South Korea, the dollar finished at 1,119 won, up from Monday's close of KRW1,117. \par \par \uc2  Against the Indonesian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar was at 8,825, little changed from late Tuesday. \par \par \uc2  Taiwan financial markets were closed Tuesday for a national holiday. \par \par \uc2  -By Netty Ismail, Dow Jones Newswires; 65-421-4807; netty.ismail@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2  1136 4250 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010820dwaa0016c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
