\par\par \b \uc2  ANALYSIS-G7 action on euro won't help Asian  \highlight22\uc2 currencies \highlight\uc2  much. \b0\par\par\uc2  By Phil Smith \par \uc2 742 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 September 2000\par 01:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2000 Reuters Limited\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Sept 25 (Reuters) - The Group of Seven's concerted \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to support the euro will give Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  some breathing space against the dollar, but any positive impact will not last long, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  It was the first combined \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  action since 1995, when the dollar hit a record low on the mark and a post-World War Two low on the yen. \par \par \uc2  Friday's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  took a thin market completely by surprise, particularly as any action on the euro was expected after this weekend's G7 meeting, not before. \par \par \uc2  "The initial impact will be long-dollar liquidation and I wouldn't be surprised to see things like dollar/sing down below 1.74 on Monday, the baht and the won should benefit but there won't be too much in the rest," said Thio Chin Loo, strategist at Paribas in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  Such long dollar liquidation is seen being much lighter in the Philippine peso, Indonesian rupiah and Taiwan dollar because there has been less speculative position-taking. \par \par \uc2  While an initial knee-jerk is on the cards, the euro's reaction to the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - estimated at $2-5 billion - was not spectacular. \par \par \uc2  After jumping some five percent against both the dollar and yen to just over 90 cents and nearly 96 yen, it came right back to end in New York on Friday around 87.70 cents and 95 yen. \par \par \uc2  "Given the (euro) market sold off so aggressively after the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  suggests people don't believe it's the end of a trend," said Steve Brice, treasury economist at Standard Chartered in Singapore. \par \par \uc2  STRONG DOLLAR \par \par \uc2  "Unless that sentiment changes it's not going to do much for regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at all...the key everyone is looking for is an end to the strong dollar environment and we certainly don't seem to have seen that yet." \par \par \uc2  Fund managers would seem to agree and were not too impressed with the central banks' action. \par \par \uc2  Asked on Friday about the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  they continued to believe there were a number of major economic factors still favouring U.S. markets over euro-zone assets. \par \par \uc2  "I've told our asset allocation committee to keep calm and keep in mind that \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the past has never been one way," said Charles Van Vleet, a senior portfolio manager at Credit Suisse Asset Management in New York. \par \par \uc2  "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  alone rarely works unless you change other policy variables such as monetary and fiscal policy also in line." \par \par \uc2  Also, Europe has had a very bad press of late with newspapers full of pictures of blockaded roads as lorry drivers and farmers bought some countries to a virtual standstill in protest over soaring fuel prices. \par \par \uc2  The subsequent perceived panic in Europe's political circles can only serve to turn investors off, analysts said. \par \par \uc2  DANISH REFERENDUM \par \par \uc2  Events next Thursday could further undermine the euro-zone in the eyes of investors when Denmark holds a referendum on whether to join the euro. \par \par \uc2  Opinion polls show the anti-euro camp at 46 percent with the pro-euro camp at 43 percent and analysts are warning a 'no' on Thursday could weaken the euro. \par \par \uc2  On top of all this, the United States is maintaining its strong dollar stance. At the G7 meeting in Prague on Saturday U.S. Treasury Secretary Lawrence Summers said a strong dollar was in the U.S. interest and said the global effort to boost the euro did not conflict with Washington's strong dollar policy. \par \par \uc2  "A strong dollar is in our national interest and that will continue to be our policy," Summers said. \par \par \uc2  All this means the dollar will keep the pressure on Asian regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly those like the Philippine peso and Indonesian rupiah where political and social factors are making matters worse. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the South Korean won and Taiwan dollar will also continue to be affected by stock market outflows. \par \par \uc2  Given the Nasdaq shed 0.66 percent on Friday any knee-jerk move higher for the won and Taiwan dollar is likely to be limited by easier stocks because of the Nasdaq drop. "If Summers is saying the U.S. still has a strong dollar policy then nothing has changed," Paribas' Thio said. \par \par \uc2  "It's very difficult for the U.S. to reverse its strong dollar policy at this point given the forthcoming election. After all, it's worked very well for them." \par \par \uc2 Dokument lba0000020010821dw9o06ndv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
