\par\par \b \uc2  US Dlr Rises Above S$1.7500; First Time Since Jul 24 \b0\par\par\uc2 496 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2000\par 10:23\par \uc2  Dow Jones International News \par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2  (Copyright (c) 2000, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.) \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE -(Dow Jones)- The U.S. dollar briefly rose above S$1.7500 late Friday, rising perilously close to the levels at which the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  was last suspected to have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in late July. \par \par \uc2  The U.S. dollar rise to a high of S$1.7505, before easing slightly on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears, dealers said \par \par \uc2  Around 0830 GMT (4:30 a.m. EDT), the U.S. dollar was trading at S$1.7498, up from S$1.7445 late Thursday in Asia. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par This is the first time since July 24 the U.S. dollar rose above\par S$1.7500. The last suspected MAS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  happened around S$1.7510\par in late July.\par - - 22/09/00 08-36G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dealers said the U.S. dollar opened on a bid tone against the\par Singapore dollar and other Asian \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on Intel Corp.'s (INTC)\par warning late Thursday that its gross margins in the third quarter will\par be around 62%, off its earlier estimates of between 63%-64%.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par It rose sharply in late afternoon, trading as its rise above 42.80\par baht triggered stop-loss buying of U.S. dollars for Singapore dollars.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's initial rise above S$1.7500 came after the start of\par trading in London, with interbank players there covering short U.S.\par dollar positions.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The U.S. dollar subsequently broke resistance at S$1.7520 in the\par absence of any signs of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by the MAS.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Dealers expect the U.S. dollar to face initial resistance at S$1.7520\par and then S$1.7550 overnight.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On the bond market, Singapore government bonds rose sharply in a\par positive reaction to MAS's decision to reopen an existing seven-year\par bond issue and to restrict the size of the additional bond offering.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par On Thursday, MAS announced it would issue an additional S$700 million\par of 4% March 2007 bonds Oct. 2.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par "The market is quite happy that (MAS) didn't come out with a new issue\par and has issued only S$700 million more (in bonds) instead of its usual\par offering of around S$2 billion," a local bond dealer said.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The yield on the 10-year benchmark government bond ended three basis\par points lower at 4.51%, while yields on the five- and seven-year bonds\par closed two basis points lower each at 3.77% and 4.18%, respectively.\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par Meanwhile, interbank money market rates were mostly steady. The\par overnight rate rose to an intraday high of 3% on fund demand ahead of\par the weekend, but eased later in the day.\par            Late Friday             Late Thursday\par            bid      offer           bid     offer\par Overnight  2.1250%  2.2500%        2.1250%  2.2500%\par 3 Month    2.4375%  2.5625%        2.4375%  2.5625%\par 1 Year     2.6875%  2.8125%        2.6875%  2.8125%\par -By Amit Prakash, Dow Jones Newswires; (+65) 421-4809;\par amit.prakash@dowjones.com\par -0- 22/09/00 09-52G\par \f28 \par \par \uc2  1163 1859 \par \par \uc2 Dokument dji0000020010805dw9m04nav\par }\par \b \uc2 Zusammenfassung der Suche\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brd